  entries: [
    {"id":"bourbon","name":"Bourbon","respell":"BUR-bun","family":"american","terms":["bourbon"],"neighbour":"tennessee-whiskey","law":"27 CFR 5.143, the United States standards of identity: bourbon whisky is made in the United States from a fermented mash of at least 51 per cent corn, distilled at no more than 80% ABV (160 proof), stored in charred new oak containers at no more than 62.5% ABV (125 proof) and bottled at 40% ABV (80 proof) or more, with no added colouring, flavouring or blending materials. Bourbon itself has no minimum age; straight bourbon needs at least two years.","made":"A mash of at least 51 per cent corn, with rye or wheat as the flavouring grain and malted barley for its enzymes, is cooked, cooled and fermented with the house's chosen yeast. It is usually distilled first in a column still and then in a doubler or thumper, and goes into new charred oak no stronger than 62.5%. Recipe, yeast, char, warehouse and years are where one house parts from another.","taste":"Vanilla, caramel and toffee from the new charred oak over sweet corn, baked apple or dark cherry and baking spice; round and sweet on the palate, drying to oak and pepper. Young bourbon runs bright and grainy, older bourbon leans to leather, tobacco and dark fruit, and a rye recipe usually reads spicier than a wheated one.","lines":{"s10":"Bourbon is American whiskey made mostly from corn and aged in new charred oak: vanilla, caramel and baking spice.","s20":"Bourbon can be made anywhere in the United States, not only Kentucky, from at least 51 per cent corn, aged in new charred oak with nothing added but water. Expect vanilla, caramel and sweet corn, then baking spice and oak.","s45":"This is bourbon, the American whiskey of corn and new oak. The federal rule asks for at least 51 per cent corn, distillation no higher than 160 proof and new charred oak barrels filled at no more than 125 proof, with nothing added but water, anywhere in the United States. Rye or wheat joins the corn, the spirit usually runs through a column still and a doubler, and the new oak gives vanilla, caramel and toffee over sweet corn and baking spice. We pour it neat, on one large cube, or in an Old Fashioned. Congress named it a distinctive product of the United States in 1964.","sale":"Bourbon is corn and new charred oak, so vanilla and caramel come first and the spice follows. Try it neat with a drop of water, or in an Old Fashioned."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn, so the vanilla and sweet corn come forward.","water":"A few drops of still water open the caramel and calm a higher proof.","rocks":"One large cube chills it slowly; small ice waters it down too fast.","highball":"Long with ginger ale or soda over ice: the corn sweetness holds up to the bubbles.","glass":"A Glencairn or tulip neat; a rocks glass for ice and the Old Fashioned; a highball glass long.","classic":"An Old Fashioned first; then a Mint Julep, Whiskey Sour, Boulevardier or Gold Rush."},"guest":[{"q":"Does bourbon have to come from Kentucky?","a":"No. The federal rule asks only that it be made in the United States. Kentucky is its home ground, but bourbon is made in many states."},{"q":"How old does bourbon have to be?","a":"Bourbon itself has no minimum age by law. Straight bourbon needs at least two years in new charred oak, and a whiskey under four years must state its age on the label."},{"q":"Is there sugar or vanilla added?","a":"No. Bourbon allows nothing but water. The vanilla, caramel and sweetness come from the corn and from the new charred oak, which gives up its sugars and vanillin to the spirit."}],"compare":"Tennessee whiskey keeps rules close to bourbon's, at least 51 per cent corn and new charred oak, but under a state law of 2013, Tennessee Code 57-2-106: made in Tennessee and filtered through maple charcoal before ageing, with one exception. The charcoal is often said to round off the new spirit; side by side the two can be close.","benchmarks":["Buffalo Trace","Wild Turkey","Four Roses","Old Forester","Jim Beam","Heaven Hill"],"shelf":["ofc-2005"],"chapters":["bourbon","american-labels"],"canon":["old-fashioned","mint-julep","whiskey-sour","boulevardier","gold-rush"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What does the federal rule require of bourbon, and what does it not require?","answer":"Made in the United States from at least 51 per cent corn, distilled at no more than 80% ABV, put into new charred oak at no more than 62.5%, bottled at 40% or more, nothing added but water. It requires neither Kentucky nor a minimum age; straight bourbon needs two years."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"What share of corn must a bourbon mash hold, at the least?","options":["51 per cent","60 per cent","75 per cent","80 per cent"],"a":0,"explain":"At least 51 per cent corn, under 27 CFR 5.143. Eighty per cent is the corn whiskey line, and corn whiskey goes into used or uncharred oak, never new charred barrels."},{"id":"q2","q":"At what strength, at most, may bourbon go into its new charred oak barrel?","options":["160 proof","125 proof","110 proof","100 proof"],"a":1,"explain":"No more than 125 proof, 62.5% ABV. 160 proof, 80% ABV, is the ceiling at the still, and 100 proof is the bottled-in-bond strength."},{"id":"q3","q":"In which year did Congress name bourbon a distinctive product of the United States?","options":["1897","1935","1964","1971"],"a":2,"explain":"1964, by a concurrent resolution agreed by both houses in May. 1897 is the year of the Bottled-in-Bond Act."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"Legal Information Institute: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/27/5.143"},{"title":"Legal Information Institute: 27 CFR 5.74, Statements of age","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/27/5.74"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: Age statement","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/age-statement/"},{"title":"United States Statutes at Large, 78 Stat. 1208: the concurrent resolution on bourbon whiskey, 1964","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/STATUTE-78/pdf/STATUTE-78-Pg1208.pdf"},{"title":"History, Art and Archives, United States House of Representatives: Designating Bourbon Whiskey","url":"https://history.house.gov/HouseRecord/Detail/15032436199"},{"title":"Distillery Trail: Bourbon declared a distinctive product of the United States","url":"https://www.distillerytrail.com/blog/when-did-bourbon-whiskey-become-a-distinctive-product-of-the-united-states/"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: Doublers and thumpers are the whiskey maker's little helpers","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/Whiskey-Distillation-Doublers-and-Thumpers-Explained"},{"title":"Alcademics: How column distillation works, bourbon edition","url":"https://alcademics.com/how-column-distillation-works-bourbon-edition/"},{"title":"Justia: Tennessee Code 57-2-106, Tennessee whiskey","url":"https://law.justia.com/codes/tennessee/2019/title-57/chapter-2/section-57-2-106/"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Bourbon","url":""},{"title":"Cask Atlas: American Labels","url":""}]},
    {"id":"wheated-bourbon","name":"Wheated bourbon","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["wheated bourbon","wheated","wheater"],"neighbour":"high-rye-bourbon","law":"No legal category of its own: a wheated bourbon is bourbon whose second grain is wheat rather than rye. The rule is bourbon's, 27 CFR 5.143: made in the United States from a mash of at least 51 per cent corn, distilled at no more than 80% ABV (160 proof), put into charred new oak at no more than 62.5% ABV (125 proof), bottled at 40% ABV or more, with no added colouring, flavouring or blending materials. Straight bourbon adds at least two years in that oak.","made":"Wheat takes the place of rye as the flavouring grain beside corn and malted barley. Published recipes run around 68 to 70 per cent corn and 16 to 20 per cent wheat, the rest malted barley: Maker's Mark's is given as 70, 16 and 14, with soft red winter wheat, and Larceny's as 68, 20 and 12. Other houses keep their figures private. Yeast, fermentation, the still, entry proof, the warehouse and age shape the spirit as much as the grain does.","taste":"Wheat is often found softer and rounder: honey, caramel, vanilla and fresh bread on the nose, soft red fruit and butterscotch on the palate, and less of the pepper and dill a rye recipe can bring. The grain is one influence among yeast, barrel, proof and age, so an older or barrel proof wheater can still run rich and hot.","lines":{"s10":"A wheated bourbon is bourbon made with wheat in place of rye beside the corn: often softer and rounder, honey and caramel rather than spice.","s20":"Wheated bourbon is still bourbon by law, at least 51 per cent corn and new charred oak, but its second grain is wheat rather than rye. It is often found softer and rounder: honey, caramel, vanilla and fresh bread, with less pepper.","s45":"This is a wheated bourbon. It is bourbon under the federal rule, made in the United States from at least 51 per cent corn and aged in new charred oak, but the second grain is wheat where most recipes use rye. Wheat is often found softer and rounder: expect honey, caramel, vanilla and fresh bread, and less of the pepper a rye recipe brings, though barrel and age matter as much. We pour it neat, with a few drops of water, or over one large cube, and it makes a gentle Old Fashioned. Stitzel-Weller, opened near Louisville in 1935, made it a house style.","sale":"Wheated bourbon trades the rye for wheat, so it is often softer and rounder: honey, caramel and fresh bread rather than spice. Try it neat with a drop of water, or in an Old Fashioned for a guest who finds bourbon sharp."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn or a small tulip, so the honey and bread come forward.","water":"A few drops of still water open the caramel and soften a higher proof.","rocks":"One large cube; small ice waters a softer whiskey down too fast.","glass":"A Glencairn or small tulip neat; a rocks glass for the cube or the Old Fashioned.","classic":"An Old Fashioned, or a Gold Rush where honey meets honey; a Whiskey Sour or Mint Julep too."},"guest":[{"q":"Is wheated bourbon sweeter?","a":"It is often found softer and rounder, with honey and bread rather than pepper, but nothing is added: bourbon allows only water. Barrel, proof and age can still make a wheater rich or hot."},{"q":"Is it the same as wheat whiskey?","a":"No. Wheated bourbon is at least 51 per cent corn with wheat as the second grain; wheat whiskey is at least 51 per cent wheat, a separate type under the same federal rule."},{"q":"Why use wheat instead of rye?","a":"It is the distiller's choice of character: rye brings spice and a drier edge, wheat a gentler, bready sweetness. Stitzel-Weller built its bourbons on wheat from 1935, and Maker's Mark chose soft red winter wheat."}],"compare":"Against a high-rye bourbon the rule is the same and only the second grain changes: wheat in place of a large share of rye. The high-rye bourbon leans to pepper, dill and a drier finish, the wheater to honey, bread and a rounder palate. Barrel and age can narrow the gap.","benchmarks":["Maker's Mark","W. L. Weller","Old Fitzgerald","Larceny","Stitzel-Weller"],"shelf":["weller-special-reserve","weller-12"],"chapters":["bourbon"],"canon":["old-fashioned","gold-rush","whiskey-sour","mint-julep"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What makes a bourbon a wheated bourbon, and is wheated bourbon a legal category?","answer":"Wheat replaces rye as the second grain beside corn and malted barley. It is no separate legal category: it is bourbon under 27 CFR 5.143, at least 51 per cent corn, charred new oak, nothing added. Often found softer and rounder, honey and bread rather than pepper."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"In a wheated bourbon, which grain does wheat take the place of?","options":["Rye","Corn","Malted barley","Oats"],"a":0,"explain":"Rye. Corn stays the main grain, at least 51 per cent by law, and malted barley stays for its enzymes; wheat takes rye's place as the flavouring grain."},{"id":"q2","q":"Which distillery, opened near Louisville in 1935, made wheated bourbon its house style?","options":["Old Forester","Heaven Hill","Stitzel-Weller","Four Roses"],"a":2,"explain":"Stitzel-Weller, opened on Derby Day 1935 in Shively, on the edge of Louisville. Heaven Hill also opened in 1935, but in Bardstown."},{"id":"q3","q":"Which wheat does Maker's Mark name as the flavouring grain of its bourbon?","options":["Hard red spring wheat","Durum wheat","Soft white wheat","Soft red winter wheat"],"a":3,"explain":"Soft red winter wheat, in place of the usual rye. Its recipe is given as 70 per cent corn, 16 per cent wheat and 14 per cent malted barley."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"Legal Information Institute: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/27/5.143"},{"title":"Maker's Mark: Maker's Mark bourbon","url":"https://www.makersmark.com/bourbons/makers-mark"},{"title":"Uproxx: What is the Maker's Mark bourbon whiskey mash bill?","url":"https://uproxx.com/life/what-is-makers-mark-mash-bill/"},{"title":"VinePair: Larceny bourbon guide","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/larceny-bourbon-guide/"},{"title":"The Bourbon Culture: Maker's Mark vs Larceny","url":"https://thebourbonculture.com/whiskey-reviews/makers-mark-bourbon-vs-larceny-bourbon-review/"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Stitzel-Weller Distillery","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stitzel-Weller"},{"title":"Whisky Magazine: A short history of Stitzel-Weller","url":"https://whiskymag.com/articles/short-history-of-stitzel-weller"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Bourbon","url":""}]},
    {"id":"high-rye-bourbon","name":"High-rye bourbon","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["high-rye bourbon","high-rye"],"neighbour":"wheated-bourbon","law":"No legal category of its own: high rye is a trade term for a bourbon with a larger share of rye than usual, and no rule fixes the line. The rule is bourbon's, 27 CFR 5.143: made in the United States from at least 51 per cent corn, distilled at no more than 80% ABV (160 proof), stored in charred new oak at no more than 62.5% ABV (125 proof), bottled at 40% ABV or more, nothing added. Straight bourbon adds at least two years.","made":"Corn stays the main grain, at least 51 per cent, with rye as the flavouring grain and malted barley for its enzymes. Most bourbon recipes carry about 8 to 15 per cent rye; a high-rye recipe is often put at about 20 to 35 per cent. Four Roses publishes one of 60 per cent corn, 35 rye and 5 malted barley; Rabbit Hole Heigold's is 70, 25 and 5, its rye malted. Yeast, still, char and age shape the spirit as much.","taste":"The rye shows as spice over bourbon's vanilla and caramel: black pepper, cinnamon, clove and a little mint or dill on the nose, a drier, spicier palate, and a warm, peppery finish. Often found brighter and leaner than a wheated bourbon, though barrel and age can round it.","lines":{"s10":"A high-rye bourbon is bourbon with a larger share of rye beside the corn: vanilla and caramel, then pepper and cinnamon.","s20":"High-rye bourbon is still bourbon, at least 51 per cent corn in new charred oak, but its rye share runs well above the usual, often a fifth to a third. Expect caramel and vanilla, then pepper, cinnamon and a drier finish.","s45":"This is a high-rye bourbon. It is bourbon under the federal rule, made in the United States from at least 51 per cent corn and aged in new charred oak, but it carries far more rye than most recipes, often a fifth to a third of the mash where many bourbons hold less than a sixth. The rye shows as black pepper, cinnamon and clove over the corn's caramel and vanilla, with a drier, spicier finish. We pour it neat, on one large cube, or in a Boulevardier, where it stands up to the Campari. Old Grand-Dad, a Kentucky brand since 1882, is a high-rye name to know.","sale":"High-rye bourbon keeps the corn's caramel and adds the rye's pepper and cinnamon, so it reads drier and livelier. Try it in a Boulevardier or a New York Sour, where the spice holds its own."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn, so the spice and the caramel both show.","water":"A few drops of still water soften the pepper and lift the fruit.","rocks":"One large cube rounds the rye without drowning it.","highball":"Long with ginger ale over ice, where the ginger meets the rye spice.","glass":"A Glencairn or tulip neat; a rocks glass for ice and the Old Fashioned.","classic":"A Boulevardier or Old Fashioned, where the spice stands up to bitters; a New York Sour or Paper Plane too."},"guest":[{"q":"Is a high-rye bourbon a rye whiskey?","a":"No. It is still bourbon, at least 51 per cent corn; rye whiskey needs at least 51 per cent rye. High rye only means a larger share of rye beside the corn."},{"q":"How much rye makes it high rye?","a":"There is no legal line. Most bourbons carry about 8 to 15 per cent rye, and the trade often calls about 20 to 35 per cent high rye. Four Roses publishes a recipe of 35 per cent."},{"q":"Will it taste spicy?","a":"Usually spicier than most bourbon: pepper, cinnamon, clove and a little mint over the corn's caramel, with a drier finish. Barrel, proof and age still shape it."}],"compare":"Against a wheated bourbon the rule is the same and only the second grain changes: a large share of rye where the wheater uses wheat. The high-rye bourbon leans to pepper, cinnamon, mint and a drier finish, the wheater to honey, fresh bread and a rounder palate. Barrel and age can narrow the gap.","benchmarks":["Four Roses","Old Grand-Dad","Basil Hayden","Rabbit Hole"],"shelf":["rabbit-hole-heigold"],"chapters":["bourbon"],"canon":["old-fashioned","boulevardier","new-york-sour","paper-plane"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What makes a bourbon a high-rye bourbon, and does any rule fix the line?","answer":"A larger share of rye than usual beside the corn, often about 20 to 35 per cent where most bourbons carry about 8 to 15. No rule fixes the line: it is bourbon under 27 CFR 5.143, at least 51 per cent corn, charred new oak, nothing added."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"What does federal law require of a bourbon called high rye?","options":["At least 20 per cent rye","At least 35 per cent rye","Nothing beyond the bourbon rule","At least 51 per cent rye"],"a":2,"explain":"High rye is a trade term with no legal line; the bourbon rule of 27 CFR 5.143 applies. At least 51 per cent rye would make it rye whiskey, not bourbon."},{"id":"q2","q":"What share of rye does Four Roses publish for the spicier of its two bourbon recipes?","options":["35 per cent","20 per cent","25 per cent","51 per cent"],"a":0,"explain":"35 per cent rye, with 60 per cent corn and 5 per cent malted barley; its other recipe is 20 per cent rye. Rabbit Hole Heigold's is 25 per cent."},{"id":"q3","q":"In which year was Old Grand-Dad, a high-rye bourbon brand, first made in Kentucky?","options":["1897","1882","1935","1964"],"a":1,"explain":"1882, when the founder named it for his grandfather, Basil Hayden. 1897 is the year of the Bottled-in-Bond Act, and 1935 the year Stitzel-Weller opened."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"A bourbon historian's blog: What do the terms high rye and low rye mean? (2011)","url":"https://chuckcowdery.blogspot.com/2011/11/what-do-terns-high-rye-and-low-rye-mean.html"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: What does the industry mean when they say high or low rye bourbon?","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/?p=45886"},{"title":"New Riff Distilling: What is high rye bourbon?","url":"https://newriffdistilling.com/what-is-high-rye-bourbon/"},{"title":"Rabbit Hole Distillery: Heigold, high rye bourbon","url":"https://www.rabbitholedistillery.com/blogs/bourbon-101/heigold-high-rye-bourbon"},{"title":"Rabbit Hole Distillery: Heigold Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","url":"https://www.rabbitholedistillery.com/products/heigold-kentucky-straight-bourbon-whiskey"},{"title":"Boozemakers: Bourbon mash bills explained","url":"https://www.boozemakers.com/articles/bourbon-mash-bills-explained"},{"title":"Four Roses: Our recipes","url":"https://fourrosesbourbon.com/our-recipes"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: How Four Roses' recipes create different bourbon profiles","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/whiskey-articles/how-four-roses-unique-recipes-create-different-bourbon-profiles/"},{"title":"VinePair: Four Roses bourbon recipe guide","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/four-roses-bourbon-recipe-guide/"},{"title":"A bourbon historian's blog: The good and not so good of Old Grand-Dad (2015)","url":"https://chuckcowdery.blogspot.com/2015/02/the-good-and-not-so-good-of-old-grand.html"},{"title":"VinePair: Old Grand-Dad bourbon guide","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/ntk-old-grand-dad-bourbon-guide/"},{"title":"Thirty One Whiskey: Basil Hayden's Kentucky Straight Bourbon Whiskey","url":"https://www.thirtyonewhiskey.com/whiskey-review-basil-haydens-kentucky-straight-bourbon-whiskey/"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Bourbon","url":""}]},
    {"id":"four-grain-bourbon","name":"Four-grain and multigrain bourbon","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["four-grain","multigrain"],"neighbour":"high-rye-bourbon","law":"No legal category of its own: four-grain or multigrain bourbon is bourbon made from more than the usual three grains, and the count on the label is the producer's. The rule is bourbon's, 27 CFR 5.143: made in the United States from at least 51 per cent corn, distilled at no more than 80% ABV (160 proof), stored in charred new oak at no more than 62.5% ABV (125 proof), bottled at 40% ABV or more, nothing added.","made":"Most bourbon uses three grains: corn, rye or wheat, and malted barley. A four-grain bourbon usually mashes rye and wheat together beside the corn and malted barley; multigrain is the wider word for any bourbon of more than three. Rabbit Hole Cavehill counts its grains another way: 70 per cent corn with malted wheat, malted barley and honey malted barley, ten per cent each, and no rye. Corn stays at least 51 per cent.","taste":"Often round and layered: honey, fresh bread and vanilla from the wheat and the oak, a gentle pepper and cinnamon where rye is in the mash, and biscuit from the malt, with a soft, warm finish. Yeast, barrel, proof and age shape it as much as the grain count does.","lines":{"s10":"A four-grain bourbon is bourbon made from four grains rather than three, usually corn, rye, wheat and malted barley.","s20":"Four-grain bourbon is still bourbon, at least 51 per cent corn in new charred oak, but it usually mashes rye and wheat together beside the malted barley. Expect honey and bread from the wheat and a little pepper from the rye.","s45":"This is a four-grain bourbon. It is bourbon under the federal rule, made in the United States from at least 51 per cent corn and aged in new charred oak, but where most recipes use three grains it uses four, usually both rye and wheat beside the corn and malted barley. Expect honey, fresh bread and vanilla from the wheat and the oak, with a gentle pepper and cinnamon from the rye, round rather than sharp. We pour it neat, on one large cube, or in an Old Fashioned. Woodford Reserve began its Master's Collection with a four-grain bourbon in 2005 and 2006.","sale":"Four-grain bourbon puts the wheat's honey and bread and the rye's spice in one glass, round and gently peppered. Try it neat with a drop of water, or in an Old Fashioned."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn, so the honey, bread and spice each show.","water":"A few drops of still water open the honey and the malt.","rocks":"One large cube; its round palate holds up as it chills.","glass":"A Glencairn or tulip neat; a rocks glass for the cube and the Old Fashioned.","classic":"An Old Fashioned; a Whiskey Sour, or a Gold Rush where the honey syrup meets the grain."},"guest":[{"q":"What are the four grains?","a":"Usually corn, rye, wheat and malted barley, where most bourbon uses three of them. Corn stays at least 51 per cent. Some houses count differently: Cavehill's four are corn and three malts, with no rye."},{"q":"Is four-grain a legal category?","a":"No. It is bourbon under the federal rule, at least 51 per cent corn, new charred oak, nothing added. Four grain describes the recipe, and the producer chooses the words."},{"q":"Does it taste different from other bourbon?","a":"Often rounder and more layered: the wheat's honey and bread, the rye's pepper, the malt's biscuit. Yeast, barrel, proof and age shape it as much as the grains do."}],"compare":"Against a high-rye bourbon the rule is the same and the grain bill differs. The high-rye bourbon puts a large share of rye beside the corn and leans to pepper and a dry finish; a four-grain bourbon spreads the small grains across rye, wheat and malt, or leaves the rye out, and usually reads softer and bready.","benchmarks":["Rabbit Hole","Woodford Reserve","Balcones"],"shelf":["rabbit-hole-cavehill"],"chapters":["bourbon"],"canon":["old-fashioned","whiskey-sour","gold-rush"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What makes a bourbon a four-grain bourbon, and is four-grain a legal category?","answer":"Four grains in place of the usual three, usually corn, rye, wheat and malted barley; multigrain is any bourbon of more than three. No legal category: it is bourbon under 27 CFR 5.143, at least 51 per cent corn, charred new oak, nothing added."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"Which grain must still make up at least 51 per cent of a four-grain bourbon?","options":["Corn","Rye","Wheat","Malted barley"],"a":0,"explain":"Corn, at least 51 per cent, as for every bourbon under 27 CFR 5.143. A whiskey of 51 per cent rye or wheat would be rye or wheat whiskey instead."},{"id":"q2","q":"Which grain does Rabbit Hole Cavehill leave out, though most four-grain bourbons use it?","options":["Malted barley","Wheat","Corn","Rye"],"a":3,"explain":"Rye. Cavehill's four are 70 per cent corn with malted wheat, malted barley and honey malted barley, ten per cent each."},{"id":"q3","q":"When did Woodford Reserve release the four-grain bourbon that began its Master's Collection?","options":["2012 and 2013","2005 and 2006","1995 and 1996","2019 and 2020"],"a":1,"explain":"Accounts give a Kentucky release in 2005 and a wider one in 2006. The mash held corn, rye, wheat and malted barley together."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"Rabbit Hole Distillery: Four grain bourbon","url":"https://www.rabbitholedistillery.com/blogs/bourbon-101/four-grain-bourbon"},{"title":"Rabbit Hole Distillery: Go Beyond the Label","url":"https://www.rabbitholedistillery.com/pages/beyond-the-label"},{"title":"The Spirits Educator: What's the Difference? Rabbit Hole Heigold vs Rabbit Hole Cavehill","url":"https://www.thespiritseducator.com/whats-the-difference/rabbit-hole-heigold-cavehill"},{"title":"Distiller: Four grain bourbon","url":"https://distiller.com/articles/four-grain-bourbon"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: Four-grain bourbon, when three grains just aren't enough","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/bourbon/7592/"},{"title":"Difford's Guide: Woodford Reserve Master's Four Grain","url":"https://www.diffordsguide.com/beer-wine-spirits/2441/woodford-reserve-masters-four-grain"},{"title":"Distiller: Woodford Reserve Master's Collection Four Grain (Batch 2)","url":"https://distiller.com/spirits/woodford-reserve-master-s-collection-four-grain-batch-2"},{"title":"Los Angeles Whiskey Society: Woodford Reserve Four Grain","url":"https://lawhiskeysociety.com/whiskey-profile/13/Woodford-Reserve-Four-Grain"},{"title":"Breaking Bourbon: Balcones Texas Pot Still Bourbon","url":"https://breakingbourbon.com/review/balcones-texas-pot-still-bourbon"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: Balcones Distilling reveals a new Texas Pot Still Bourbon","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/bourbon/balcones-distilling-reveals-a-new-texas-pot-still-bourbon/"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Bourbon","url":""}]},
    {"id":"straight-whiskey","name":"Straight whiskey","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["straight whiskey","straight bourbon","straight rye"],"neighbour":"bottled-in-bond","law":"27 CFR 5.143, the United States standards of identity: straight bourbon, rye, wheat, malt or rye malt whisky meets the standard of its type and is stored at least two years in charred new oak, with no added colouring, flavouring or blending materials; straight corn whisky takes its two years in used or uncharred new oak. Straights of one type mixed together stay straight only when all were made in the same state. Under four years, the label states the age (27 CFR 5.74).","made":"Straight is a matter of time and purity, not of recipe. The whiskey is made to its type, bourbon or rye at least 51 per cent of its grain, filled into new charred oak at no more than 62.5% ABV and left at least two years; only water goes in at bottling. A batch may marry many barrels, and many distilleries' straights of one type, so long as all were made in one state; across states the label reads a blend of straight whiskies.","taste":"Straight promises time and purity, not one flavour. A straight bourbon reads vanilla, caramel and baking spice over sweet corn; a straight rye, pepper, mint and rye bread. Two years in new oak gives colour and a first layer of vanilla; four years and more deepen it to toffee, leather and dried fruit, with a firmer, drying finish.","lines":{"s10":"Straight whiskey is American whiskey aged at least two years in new charred oak with nothing added, as in straight bourbon or straight rye.","s20":"Straight whiskey is bourbon, rye or another American type rested at least two years in new charred oak, with no colouring or flavouring added; under four years, the label states its age. Expect fuller oak, vanilla and caramel.","s45":"This is a straight whiskey, here a straight bourbon. Straight is a federal term: the whiskey meets the rule for its type, then rests at least two years in new charred oak, with no colouring, flavouring or blending materials added, and a bottle under four years must state its age. Barrels of one type can be married only from one state. The years in new oak give vanilla, caramel and toffee over the grain, with baking spice and a drying finish. We pour it neat, with a few drops of water, or in a Manhattan. Bottled-in-bond, the law of 1897, asks more: four years and 100 proof.","sale":"Straight on an American label means at least two years in new charred oak and nothing added but water. Try a straight bourbon neat, or a straight rye in a Manhattan or a Sazerac."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn, so the oak and the grain both show.","water":"A few drops of still water open the caramel and soften the oak.","rocks":"One large cube for a guest who likes it cold; it melts slowly.","highball":"A younger straight goes long with ginger ale or soda over ice.","glass":"A Glencairn or tulip neat; a rocks glass for the cube and the Old Fashioned; a coupe for the Manhattan.","classic":"An Old Fashioned or a Manhattan with straight bourbon or rye; a Sazerac with straight rye."},"guest":[{"q":"Does straight mean it came from one barrel?","a":"No. Straight is about age and purity: at least two years in new charred oak, nothing added. A straight whiskey may marry many barrels; single barrel is a separate claim on the label."},{"q":"How old is a straight bourbon?","a":"At least two years in new charred oak. Under four years the label must state the age, so a straight bourbon with no age statement is at least four years old."},{"q":"Can a straight whiskey be a blend?","a":"Yes, of straights of one type from one state, and it stays straight. Straights from different states make a blend of straight whiskies, and the label must say so."}],"compare":"Bottled-in-bond, 27 CFR 5.88, asks more than straight: one distilling season, one distiller at one distillery, at least four years in a bonded warehouse, exactly 100 proof and the distillery on the label. In the glass a bonded bottle is always 100 proof and four years or more; a straight can be younger, lighter or stronger.","benchmarks":["Four Roses","Buffalo Trace","W. L. Weller","Rabbit Hole","Thomas H. Handy"],"shelf":["ofc-2005"],"chapters":["american-labels","bourbon"],"canon":["old-fashioned","manhattan","sazerac"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What does straight whiskey on an American label promise, and what does it not promise?","answer":"Under 27 CFR 5.143: a whiskey of its type stored at least two years in charred new oak, used or uncharred oak for straight corn, nothing added but water, and a mix of one type only from one state. Under four years it states its age. It promises neither one barrel nor bottled-in-bond."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"How long, at the least, must a straight bourbon rest in charred new oak?","options":["One year","Four years","Two years","Three years"],"a":2,"explain":"Two years, under 27 CFR 5.143. Four years is the bottled-in-bond minimum, and also the age below which a whiskey must state its age on the label."},{"id":"q2","q":"Straight bourbons from two different states are mixed together. How must the label read?","options":["A blend of straight whiskies","Straight bourbon whiskey","Bottled-in-bond bourbon","Light whiskey"],"a":0,"explain":"A blend of straight whiskies, often written blended straight bourbon whiskies. Straights of one type stay straight only when all were made in the same state."},{"id":"q3","q":"In what kind of oak does straight corn whisky take its two years?","options":["Charred new oak","Used or uncharred new oak","French oak only","Ex-sherry casks only"],"a":1,"explain":"Used or uncharred new oak. Corn whisky is never treated with charred wood, so straight corn is the one straight type kept out of charred new oak."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"Legal Information Institute: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/27/5.143"},{"title":"Legal Information Institute: 27 CFR 5.74, Statements of age","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/27/5.74"},{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.88, Bottled in bond","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-F/section-5.88"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: Age statement","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/age-statement/"},{"title":"Breaking Bourbon: What is bourbon? Down the rabbit hole of America's native spirit","url":"https://www.breakingbourbon.com/article/what-is-bourbon-down-the-rabbit-hole-of-americas-native-spirit"},{"title":"Alcademics: What is a blended straight bourbon whiskey?","url":"https://alcademics.com/?p=3056"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: What does straight bourbon, or straight whiskey, really mean?","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/american-whiskey/what-does-straight-bourbon-or-straight-whiskey-really-mean/"},{"title":"Distiller magazine: What the Bottled-in-Bond Act of 1897 means today","url":"https://distilling.com/distillermagazine/what-the-bottled-in-bond-act-of-1897-means-today/"},{"title":"Distillery Trail: What is the Bottled-in-Bond Act of 1897?","url":"https://distillerytrail.com/?p=8116"},{"title":"Four Roses: Our recipes","url":"https://fourrosesbourbon.com/our-recipes"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: American Labels","url":""},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Bourbon","url":""}]},
    {"id":"bottled-in-bond","name":"Bottled-in-bond","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["bottled-in-bond","bonded"],"neighbour":"straight-whiskey","law":"27 CFR 5.88, the United States rule that grew from the Bottled-in-Bond Act of 1897: spirits of one kind, made in one distilling season (January to June, or July to December) by one distiller at one distillery, stored at least four years in wood in a bonded warehouse, changed by nothing but pure water and bottled at exactly 100 proof (50% ABV). The label names the distillery and, if it differs, the place of bottling.","made":"Bonded is a promise laid over a whiskey of its own type, most often bourbon or rye. Everything in the bottle was distilled in one half-year season at one distillery, filled to its type's rules and left at least four years in a bonded warehouse; many barrels from that season may be married. At bottling it is cut with water to exactly 100 proof, with no colouring or flavouring. Age beyond four years, recipe and warehouse vary by house.","taste":"Bonded promises a known strength and age, not one flavour. At 100 proof and four years or more, a bonded bourbon reads caramel, vanilla and toasted oak over baking spice; a bonded rye, pepper, mint and rye bread. Fuller and warmer than an 80 or 90 proof bottling, with a longer finish that holds through ice, sugar and citrus.","lines":{"s10":"Bottled-in-bond is American whiskey of one season and one distillery, aged at least four years and bottled at exactly 100 proof.","s20":"Bottled-in-bond goes back to an 1897 law: one distilling season, one distiller at one distillery, at least four years in a bonded warehouse, bottled at exactly 100 proof. Expect caramel, oak and spice at a full, steady strength.","s45":"This is a bottled-in-bond whiskey, here a bonded bourbon. Bonded is a federal standard, 27 CFR 5.88: everything in the bottle was made in one distilling season by one distiller at one distillery, aged at least four years in a bonded warehouse and bottled at exactly 100 proof, with only water added, and the label names the distillery. At that strength it carries caramel, vanilla and toasted oak over baking spice, warm and long. We pour it neat with a few drops of water, or in an Old Fashioned. The rule began with the Bottled-in-Bond Act of 1897, when the government stood behind the bottle.","sale":"Bottled-in-bond is a promise kept since 1897: one season, one distillery, four years at least and exactly 100 proof. Try it neat with a drop of water, or in an Old Fashioned that keeps its backbone."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn; at 100 proof the oak and spice come through whole.","water":"A few drops of still water soften the heat and open the caramel and fruit.","rocks":"One large cube; the strength holds up as it slowly melts.","highball":"Long with soda or ginger ale over ice; at 100 proof the whiskey still leads.","glass":"A Glencairn or tulip neat; a rocks glass for the cube and the Old Fashioned; a coupe for the Manhattan.","classic":"An Old Fashioned first; the strength also carries a Manhattan or a Whiskey Sour."},"guest":[{"q":"Does bonded mean it is better?","a":"Not by itself. Bonded guarantees how it was made: one season, one distillery, at least four years and exactly 100 proof. Whether it suits you is a matter of taste, and we can taste to find out."},{"q":"Is a bonded bourbon from a single barrel?","a":"No. It must all come from one distilling season at one distillery, but many barrels from that season may be married. Single barrel is a separate claim on the label."},{"q":"How old is a bonded whiskey?","a":"At least four years in wood. Because it is four years or more, the label need not state an age, though some bonded bottles do."}],"compare":"Straight whiskey, 27 CFR 5.143, asks two years in new charred oak and nothing added, at 40% ABV or more, and may marry many seasons and distilleries of one state. Bonded narrows it: one season, one distillery, four years and exactly 100 proof. A bonded bottle is always 100 proof; a straight may be younger and softer, or stronger.","benchmarks":["Old Fitzgerald","Old Grand-Dad","Evan Williams","Rittenhouse","E. H. Taylor","Heaven Hill"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["american-labels"],"canon":["old-fashioned","manhattan","whiskey-sour"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What does bottled-in-bond promise on an American whiskey label, and what does it not promise?","answer":"Under 27 CFR 5.88: one distilling season, one distiller at one distillery, at least four years in wood in a bonded warehouse, nothing added but water, bottled at exactly 100 proof, with the distillery named on the label. It promises neither a single barrel nor a better whiskey."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"At what strength must a bottled-in-bond whiskey go into the bottle?","options":["80 proof (40% ABV)","100 proof (50% ABV)","90 proof (45% ABV)","125 proof (62.5% ABV)"],"a":1,"explain":"Exactly 100 proof, 50% ABV, under 27 CFR 5.88. 125 proof is the ceiling for bourbon going into its barrel, and 80 proof the floor for bottling any whiskey."},{"id":"q2","q":"In which year did Congress pass the Bottled-in-Bond Act?","options":["1897","1920","1933","1964"],"a":0,"explain":"1897, on 3 March. Prohibition began in 1920 and ended in 1933, and in 1964 Congress named bourbon a distinctive product of the United States."},{"id":"q3","q":"A bonded whiskey must all be distilled in one distilling season. How long is a distilling season?","options":["One calendar year, January to December","One calendar month of distilling","January to June, or July to December","Any four years in a row"],"a":2,"explain":"Half a year: January to June or July to December, under 27 CFR 5.1. Every drop in a bonded bottle was distilled within one of those two seasons, at one distillery."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.88, Bottled in bond","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-F/section-5.88"},{"title":"Legal Information Institute: 27 CFR 5.88, Bottled in bond","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/27/5.88"},{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.1, Definitions (distilling season)","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-A/section-5.1"},{"title":"Legal Information Institute: 27 CFR 5.74, Statements of age","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/27/5.74"},{"title":"Distiller magazine: What the Bottled-in-Bond Act of 1897 means today","url":"https://distilling.com/distillermagazine/what-the-bottled-in-bond-act-of-1897-means-today/"},{"title":"Distillery Trail: What is the Bottled-in-Bond Act of 1897?","url":"https://www.distillerytrail.com/blog/what-is-bottled-in-bond/"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Bottled in bond","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bottled_in_bond"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: Bottled in bond bourbon (and Tennessee whiskey) is back","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/bottled-in-bond-bourbon-whisky-list/"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: American Labels","url":""}]},
    {"id":"small-batch-single-barrel","name":"Small batch and single barrel","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["small batch","single barrel"],"neighbour":"barrel-proof","law":"No legal category of its own: neither small batch nor single barrel is defined in the United States standards of identity, and no rule sets a barrel count. Small batch is the producer's word for a smaller mingling of chosen barrels; single barrel means one cask bottled alone. The whiskey still meets its type under 27 CFR 5.143, bourbon or rye, and the label's type, age and proof carry the legal facts.","made":"For small batch the producer chooses fewer barrels than its standard bottling, often from chosen warehouse floors or ages, and mingles them; the count is its own, from a few barrels to a couple of hundred. For single barrel one cask is emptied and bottled alone, cut with water to a set proof or left at barrel strength; the label often carries the barrel number, warehouse and bottling date.","taste":"Neither term names a flavour. Small batch is usually blended toward a house profile, so one bottle tastes much like the next; single barrel shows one cask, so two bottles of one brand can differ, one more vanilla and caramel, the next more oak, pepper or dark fruit.","lines":{"s10":"Small batch means a chosen mingling of fewer barrels; single barrel means one cask, bottled alone, each barrel a little different.","s20":"Small batch and single barrel are producers' words, not federal rules: small batch is a smaller mingling of chosen barrels, single barrel one cask bottled alone. Expect the house character, and from a single barrel, that cask's own turn of oak and spice.","s45":"This is a single barrel bourbon, and small batch is its close relation. Neither is a legal category: the whiskey meets its type, here bourbon under 27 CFR 5.143, and the label adds how many barrels went in. Small batch mingles a chosen few barrels, the count the producer's own; single barrel bottles one cask alone, often with its barrel number on the label. Each cask reads its own way, vanilla and caramel in one, more oak, pepper and dark fruit in the next. We pour it neat with a few drops of water, or on one cube. Blanton's, from 1984, is usually named the first modern single barrel bourbon.","sale":"Single barrel is one cask in the bottle, so each barrel has its own turn of oak and spice. Try it neat with a few drops of water, or in an Old Fashioned."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn, so one cask's character shows plainly.","water":"A few drops of still water open the caramel and soften a higher proof.","rocks":"One large cube for a guest who likes it cold; it melts slowly.","glass":"A Glencairn or tulip neat; a rocks glass for the cube and the Old Fashioned; a coupe for the Manhattan.","classic":"An Old Fashioned or a Manhattan; a single barrel is usually tasted neat first."},"guest":[{"q":"Is small batch a legal term?","a":"No. No federal rule defines it or sets a barrel count; it is the producer's word for a smaller mingling of chosen barrels. The type, age and proof on the label carry the facts."},{"q":"Will this single barrel taste like the last bottle I had?","a":"Not exactly. Each cask ages in its own way with its wood and its place in the warehouse, so one barrel reads sweeter, another spicier. That difference is the point of single barrel."},{"q":"Is single barrel better than small batch?","a":"Neither is better by rule. Single barrel shows one cask as it is; small batch mingles a few for a steadier house character. It comes down to which you enjoy."}],"compare":"Barrel proof names a strength, the whiskey bottled at about the proof it left the cask; small batch and single barrel name how many barrels went in. They can meet, a single barrel bottled at barrel proof, but many small batch and single barrel bottles are cut with water to a set proof, so read the strength on the label.","benchmarks":["Blanton's","Four Roses","Booker's","Knob Creek","Elijah Craig","Eagle Rare"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["american-labels","blending"],"canon":["old-fashioned","manhattan"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What do small batch and single barrel promise on an American whiskey label, and does the law define them?","answer":"Neither is defined in federal law. Small batch is the producer's word for a smaller mingling of chosen barrels, with no fixed count; single barrel means one cask bottled alone, so bottles differ from barrel to barrel. The type, age and proof still come from the rest of the label."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"How many barrels does federal law allow in a small batch bourbon?","options":["No number: the law does not define it","No more than ten barrels","No more than twenty barrels","No more than one hundred barrels"],"a":0,"explain":"No federal rule defines small batch or sets a count. It is the producer's own word, and batches run from a few barrels to a couple of hundred."},{"id":"q2","q":"Which brand, launched in 1984, is usually named the first modern single barrel bourbon?","options":["Booker's","Knob Creek","Blanton's","Maker's Mark"],"a":2,"explain":"Blanton's, from the Frankfort distillery that is now Buffalo Trace, in 1984. Booker's and Knob Creek are small batch bourbons of the late 1980s and early 1990s."},{"id":"q3","q":"What does single barrel on a bourbon label tell you?","options":["It is bottled at barrel proof","It is at least four years old","It came from one distilling season","Every drop came from one cask"],"a":3,"explain":"One cask, bottled alone. Single barrel says nothing fixed about proof or age; one distilling season is the bottled-in-bond rule."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"Gear Patrol: This bourbon term is everywhere. But does it really mean anything?","url":"https://www.gearpatrol.com/food/drinks/a36688708/small-batch-bourbon-whiskey/"},{"title":"Paste Magazine: Small batch vs single barrel bourbon, the definitions","url":"https://www.pastemagazine.com/drink/whiskey/small-batch-vs-single-barrel-bourbon-definition"},{"title":"The Daily Meal: What the term small batch bourbon really means","url":"https://www.thedailymeal.com/1546174/small-batch-bourbon-meaning"},{"title":"Heaven Hill: Decipher a whiskey label like a pro","url":"https://blog.heavenhilldistillery.com/decipher-whiskey-label-like-pro/"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Blanton's","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blanton%27s"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: More than a horse stopper, the history of Blanton's bourbon","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/whiskey-articles/more-than-a-horse-stopper-the-revolutionary-history-of-blantons-bourbon/"},{"title":"Tasting Table: How Blanton's revolutionized the bourbon industry","url":"https://tastingtable.com/stories/how-blantons-revolutionized-the-bourbon-industry"},{"title":"Knob Creek, Beam founders: the Small Batch Collection","url":"https://founders.beamsuntory.com/dna/knobcreek"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: American Labels","url":""},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Blending and the language of the label","url":""}]},
    {"id":"barrel-proof","name":"Barrel proof","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["barrel proof","cask strength"],"neighbour":"bottled-in-bond","law":"27 CFR 5.87, the United States labelling rule: barrel proof or cask strength may describe spirits stored in wood barrels only when they are bottled no more than two degrees of proof (1% ABV) below their strength when dumped from the barrel. Original proof, original barrel proof, original cask strength and entry proof mean bottled at the same proof the spirit went into the barrel. The whiskey still meets its type, bourbon or rye, under 27 CFR 5.143.","made":"The whiskey is made and aged to its type, bourbon filled into new charred oak at no more than 125 proof. In the warehouse the strength moves: in hot upper floors water leaves through the oak faster than alcohol and the proof usually climbs; lower and cooler, it climbs less or holds. At dumping the barrels go into the bottle with little or no water, so each batch has its own proof.","taste":"Concentrated rather than different: the whiskey's own flavours at full volume. A barrel proof bourbon reads toffee, vanilla and dark cherry over cinnamon and charred oak, thick and oily on the tongue, with heat that builds and a long, drying finish. Water calms the alcohol and often brings fruit and caramel forward.","lines":{"s10":"Barrel proof, or cask strength, is whiskey bottled at about the strength it came out of the barrel, with little or no water added.","s20":"Barrel proof means bottled within two degrees of proof of the strength at which the whiskey left the barrel, by federal rule. Each batch has its own proof; expect thick toffee, dark cherry and oak, hot until water opens it.","s45":"This is a barrel proof bourbon, also called cask strength. The federal rule, 27 CFR 5.87, allows the words only when the whiskey is bottled no more than two degrees of proof below the strength it had leaving the barrel, so little or no water goes in and each batch carries its own proof. Years in a hot warehouse often push the proof up. Expect toffee, dark cherry, cinnamon and oak, thick and warm, opening with a few drops of water. We pour it neat with water on the side, or on one cube. Booker's, first sold uncut in the late 1980s, helped make barrel proof bourbon a style.","sale":"Barrel proof is the whiskey at the strength it left the barrel, so every flavour comes concentrated. Taste it neat and add water a drop at a time until it opens."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn, with a small jug of still water beside it.","water":"A few drops at a time calm the heat and open the caramel, fruit and spice.","rocks":"One large cube tames the proof slowly; never a glass full of small ice.","glass":"A Glencairn or small tulip neat; a rocks glass for the cube or the Old Fashioned.","classic":"An Old Fashioned stirred a little longer; a barrel proof bourbon also gives an Amaretto Sour its backbone."},"guest":[{"q":"Why is it so strong?","a":"Almost nothing was added at bottling: it went into the bottle at about the strength it came out of the barrel, often well above 50% ABV. A few drops of water bring it to your taste."},{"q":"Should I add water?","a":"Many guests do. Start with a few drops, taste, and add more if you like; water calms the heat and often opens caramel and fruit that the alcohol was hiding."},{"q":"Is barrel proof the same as cask strength?","a":"On an American label, yes: the rule treats them as one, bottled within two degrees of proof of the strength at dumping. Scotch and Irish bottlers usually say cask strength."}],"compare":"Bottled-in-bond, 27 CFR 5.88, fixes the strength at exactly 100 proof, cut with water, after one season and at least four years at one distillery. Barrel proof sets no age and no number: the whiskey is bottled within two degrees of the proof it left the barrel at, so it is usually stronger, and each batch differs.","benchmarks":["George T. Stagg","Booker's","Elijah Craig","Wild Turkey","Maker's Mark","Larceny"],"shelf":["george-t-stagg","stagg"],"chapters":["american-labels","maturation"],"canon":["old-fashioned","amaretto-sour"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What does barrel proof, or cask strength, promise on an American label, and how does it differ from entry proof?","answer":"Under 27 CFR 5.87, barrel proof or cask strength means a spirit stored in wood barrels and bottled no more than two degrees of proof below its strength when dumped. Original proof or entry proof means bottled at the same proof it went into the barrel. Neither sets an age."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"Under the federal rule, how far below its dumping strength may a barrel proof whiskey be bottled?","options":["No more than two degrees of proof","No more than ten degrees of proof","No more than twenty degrees of proof","Any amount, if the label states it"],"a":0,"explain":"No more than two degrees of proof, 1% ABV, under 27 CFR 5.87. The small allowance covers the slight drop between gauging the barrels and bottling."},{"id":"q2","q":"A whiskey label reads entry proof. What does that promise?","options":["Bottled at exactly 100 proof after four years","Bottled within two degrees of its dumping strength","Bottled at the strength it went into the barrel","Bottled at 125 proof, the bourbon barrel ceiling"],"a":2,"explain":"Entry proof, like original proof, means the bottling proof equals the proof at which the spirit entered the barrel. Within two degrees of dumping strength is barrel proof; exactly 100 proof is bottled-in-bond."},{"id":"q3","q":"In the hot upper floors of a Kentucky warehouse, what usually happens to a barrel's proof over the years?","options":["It falls steadily","It climbs","It stays exactly at entry proof","It drops to 80 proof"],"a":1,"explain":"It usually climbs: in the heat, water leaves through the oak faster than alcohol. Lower and cooler floors raise it less, and some barrels hold close to their entry proof."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.87, Barrel proof and similar terms","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-F/section-5.87"},{"title":"Legal Information Institute: 27 CFR 5.87, Barrel proof and similar terms","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/27/5.87"},{"title":"TTB: ATF Ruling 79-9, original proof, entry proof and barrel proof","url":"https://ttb.gov/images/pdfs/rulings/79-9.htm"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: Learn the basics of barrel proof whiskey","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/learn-the-basics-of-barrel-proof-whiskey"},{"title":"VinePair: Barrel proof, cask strength and full proof whiskey, the differences explained","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/barrel-proof-cask-strength-full-proof-whiskey-differences-explained"},{"title":"Kentucky Bourbon Trail: Summer in a warehouse","url":"https://kybourbon.com/enthusiast-stories/summer-in-a-warehouse/"},{"title":"Distiller: A look at how weather affects whiskey","url":"https://distiller.com/articles/weather-affects-whiskey"},{"title":"The Bourbon Culture: A statistical analysis of every warehouse at the Brown-Forman distillery","url":"https://thebourbonculture.com/whiskey-info/a-statistical-analysis-of-every-warehouse-at-the-brown-forman-distillery/"},{"title":"Beam Distilling: About Booker's","url":"https://www.beamdistilling.com/bookersbourbon/about-bookers"},{"title":"The Daily Meal: Booker's bourbon, don't just throw it back","url":"https://www.thedailymeal.com/drink/booker-s-bourbon-don-t-just-throw-it-back"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: 14 barrel proof whiskeys that deliver bold flavor","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/14-barrel-proof-whiskeys-that-deliver-bold-flavor"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: American Labels","url":""},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Oak, time and the warehouse","url":""}]},
    {"id":"tennessee-whiskey","name":"Tennessee whiskey","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["Tennessee whiskey","Tennessee"],"neighbour":"bourbon","law":"Tennessee Code 57-2-106, the state law of 2013: Tennessee whiskey is made in Tennessee from at least 51 per cent corn, distilled at no more than 80% ABV (160 proof), filtered through maple charcoal before ageing, aged in new charred oak barrels filled at no more than 62.5% ABV (125 proof), and bottled at 40% ABV (80 proof) or more. One exception, drawn by county and date, frees a single distillery from the charcoal step.","made":"A mash of at least 51 per cent corn, most often with rye and malted barley, is fermented and distilled much as bourbon is. Before the barrel, the new spirit runs slowly through a deep vat of sugar maple charcoal, the Lincoln County Process; George Dickel chills it first. Then come new charred oak, years in the warehouse and water to bottling strength. Benjamin Prichard's, under the law's one exception, skips the charcoal.","taste":"Close to bourbon: sweet corn, caramel and vanilla from the new oak, often a note of ripe banana, then toasted oak and a little spice. The charcoal is often said to take the rough edges off the new spirit, so many find it softer and rounder through the middle, with a gentle finish.","lines":{"s10":"Tennessee whiskey is corn-led whiskey made in Tennessee and filtered through maple charcoal before it goes into new charred oak.","s20":"Tennessee whiskey follows a state law of 2013: made in Tennessee from at least 51 per cent corn, filtered through maple charcoal before ageing, aged in new charred oak. Expect caramel, vanilla and sweet corn, soft and round.","s45":"This is Tennessee whiskey, the state's own style. A Tennessee law of 2013 asks what bourbon asks, at least 51 per cent corn and new charred oak among it, and two things more: it is made in Tennessee, and the new spirit runs through maple charcoal before ageing, the Lincoln County Process, with one exception. The charcoal is often said to round the spirit: caramel, vanilla and sweet corn, a touch of banana and toasted oak, soft through the finish. We pour it on the rocks, neat, or long with ginger ale. The process is named for Lincoln County, home to Lynchburg until 1871.","sale":"Tennessee whiskey is bourbon's neighbour with one more step: it drips through maple charcoal before it ever meets the barrel. Try it over ice, or long with ginger ale."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn, so the caramel and sweet corn come forward.","water":"A few drops of still water open the fruit and soften the oak.","rocks":"On the rocks is a natural pour; one large cube keeps it from watering down fast.","highball":"Long with ginger ale over ice, a Whiskey Ginger; soda for a drier glass.","glass":"A Glencairn neat; a rocks glass for ice, the Old Fashioned and the sour; a highball glass long.","classic":"A Whiskey Ginger or an Old Fashioned; a Whiskey Sour for a guest who wants citrus."},"guest":[{"q":"Is Tennessee whiskey just bourbon?","a":"It keeps bourbon's rules for grain, still and barrel, but Tennessee law gives it its own name: made in Tennessee and filtered through maple charcoal before ageing. Its makers label it Tennessee whiskey."},{"q":"What does the charcoal do?","a":"The new spirit drips through sugar maple charcoal before it goes into the barrel. It is often said to take off harsh edges and round the spirit; the barrel then gives the caramel and vanilla."},{"q":"Does it all come from Lynchburg?","a":"No, from anywhere in Tennessee. Lynchburg is home to Jack Daniel's; George Dickel, Nelson's Green Brier, Benjamin Prichard's and others distil elsewhere in the state."}],"compare":"Bourbon, 27 CFR 5.143, may be made anywhere in the United States and asks no charcoal. Tennessee whiskey keeps bourbon's grain, still and barrel limits, but must be made in Tennessee and filtered through maple charcoal before ageing, with one exception. Side by side the Tennessee glass is often found a little softer; the two can be close.","benchmarks":["Jack Daniel's","George Dickel","Nelson's Green Brier","Uncle Nearest","Benjamin Prichard's"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["american-labels","bourbon"],"canon":["old-fashioned","whiskey-sour","whiskey-ginger"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What does Tennessee law ask of Tennessee whiskey beyond bourbon's rules, and what is its exception?","answer":"Under Tennessee Code 57-2-106 of 2013: made in Tennessee and filtered through maple charcoal before ageing, the Lincoln County Process, beside at least 51 per cent corn, 160 proof at most off the still, new charred oak filled at no more than 125 proof and bottling at 80 proof or more. One exception frees Benjamin Prichard's from the charcoal."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"What step does Tennessee law add before Tennessee whiskey goes into the barrel?","options":["Distilling three times in pot stills","Filtering through maple charcoal","Resting in used sherry casks","Adding a little caramel colouring"],"a":1,"explain":"Filtering the new spirit through maple charcoal, the Lincoln County Process, under Tennessee Code 57-2-106. Nothing may be added, and the barrels are new charred oak, as for bourbon."},{"id":"q2","q":"In which year did Tennessee write its definition of Tennessee whiskey into state law?","options":["1871","1897","1964","2013"],"a":3,"explain":"2013, Tennessee Code 57-2-106. Lynchburg's county, Moore, was drawn in 1871, the Bottled-in-Bond Act passed in 1897, and Congress named bourbon a distinctive product of the United States in 1964."},{"id":"q3","q":"Which Tennessee distillery does the exception in the 2013 law free from the charcoal step?","options":["Jack Daniel's","George Dickel","Benjamin Prichard's","Nelson's Green Brier"],"a":2,"explain":"Benjamin Prichard's, in Lincoln County, which made its whiskey without the charcoal step before the law. The exception is drawn by county and date, and the state's attorney general read it in 2015 as covering one distillery."}],"sources":[{"title":"Justia: Tennessee Code 57-2-106, Tennessee whiskey","url":"https://law.justia.com/codes/tennessee/2019/title-57/chapter-2/section-57-2-106/"},{"title":"FindLaw: Tennessee Code 57-2-106","url":"https://codes.findlaw.com/tn/title-57-intoxicating-liquors/tn-code-sect-57-2-106/"},{"title":"Tennessee Attorney General: opinion 15-23 on the Tennessee whiskey statute","url":"https://www.tn.gov/content/dam/tn/attorneygeneral/documents/ops/2015/op15-23.pdf"},{"title":"Associated Press, via KSL: the exemption in the Tennessee whiskey law","url":"https://www.ksl.com/article/33886270"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Tennessee whiskey","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tennessee_whiskey"},{"title":"Alcohol Professor: Chalk one up for the charcoal","url":"https://www.alcoholprofessor.com/blog-posts/blog/2013/05/13/chalk-one-up-for-the-charcoal"},{"title":"Distiller: Lincoln County Process, charcoal mellowing in Tennessee","url":"https://distiller.com/articles/lincoln-county-process"},{"title":"Tasting Table: Jack Daniel's vs George Dickel, how the Tennessee whiskeys differ","url":"https://www.tastingtable.com/1521469/jack-daniels-george-dickel-whiskey-differ/"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Lynchburg, Tennessee","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynchburg,_Tennessee"},{"title":"Tennessee Encyclopedia: Moore County","url":"https://tennesseeencyclopedia.net/?p=1295"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: American Labels","url":""},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Bourbon","url":""}]},
    {"id":"kentucky-rye","name":"Kentucky-style rye","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["Kentucky-style rye","Kentucky rye"],"neighbour":"monongahela-rye","law":"No legal category of its own: Kentucky-style names a recipe, not a rule. The whiskey is rye whisky under 27 CFR 5.143, the United States standards of identity: made from at least 51 per cent rye, distilled at no more than 80% ABV (160 proof), stored in charred new oak at no more than 62.5% ABV (125 proof) and bottled at 40% ABV (80 proof) or more. Straight rye adds at least two years and nothing added.","made":"The mash sits just over the legal line, often about 51 to 55 per cent rye, with corn making most of the rest and malted barley for its enzymes, much like a bourbon recipe with the two leading grains turned round. It is cooked, fermented and distilled on the same column stills and doublers as the distillery's bourbon, then aged in new charred oak; many are bottled as straight rye.","taste":"Rye spice over a rounder corn base: black pepper, cinnamon and mint over caramel, vanilla and honeyed corn, with rye bread and dried herbs on the finish. Usually softer and sweeter than a rye-heavy Pennsylvania rye, and close in feel to a high-rye bourbon.","lines":{"s10":"Kentucky-style rye is rye whiskey made just over the 51 per cent rye line, with plenty of corn: spicy, but round.","s20":"Kentucky-style rye is a recipe, not a rule: rye whisky at about 51 to 55 per cent rye, the rest mostly corn, aged in new charred oak. Expect pepper, cinnamon and mint over caramel and sweet corn.","s45":"This is a Kentucky-style rye. American rye whisky must be at least 51 per cent rye and aged in new charred oak, by federal rule; Kentucky style sits just over that line, often about 51 to 55 per cent rye, with corn making most of the rest and a little malted barley, distilled on the same stills as bourbon. So the rye's black pepper, cinnamon and mint ride on caramel, vanilla and sweet corn, rounder than a rye-heavy Pennsylvania rye. We pour it neat, or in a Sazerac or a Manhattan. In New Orleans the Sazerac turned from cognac to American rye when cognac grew scarce.","sale":"Kentucky-style rye keeps rye's pepper and mint but rounds them with corn, so it holds its spice in a stirred drink without turning harsh. Try it in a Sazerac, the New Orleans way, or in a Manhattan."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn, so the pepper and the corn sweetness both show.","water":"A few drops of still water calm the spice and bring out caramel and mint.","rocks":"One large cube for a cooler pour; the corn keeps it round as it melts.","highball":"Long with ginger ale over ice; the rye spice stands up to the ginger.","glass":"A Glencairn neat; a chilled rocks glass for the Sazerac; a coupe for the Manhattan.","classic":"A Sazerac first; then a Manhattan, a Vieux Carr\u00e9 or a Cocktail \u00e0 la Louisiane."},"guest":[{"q":"What makes it Kentucky style?","a":"The recipe: just over the 51 per cent rye the law asks, with plenty of corn and a little malted barley. The corn makes it rounder and sweeter than a rye-heavy Pennsylvania or Maryland rye."},{"q":"Is it sweeter than other ryes?","a":"Often a little, yes. The corn adds caramel and roundness under the spice. A rye made mostly or wholly of rye usually reads drier, grassier and more peppery."},{"q":"How is it different from a high-rye bourbon?","a":"By which grain leads: rye whisky is at least 51 per cent rye, bourbon at least 51 per cent corn. A Kentucky-style rye and a high-rye bourbon can sit close together in the glass."}],"compare":"Monongahela and Maryland rye are historic regional styles, no more a legal category than Kentucky style: Pennsylvania's rye was usually rye-heavy with little or no corn, and Maryland's is often described as softer. Against them a Kentucky-style rye carries more corn, so it reads rounder and sweeter, its spice riding on caramel.","benchmarks":["Rittenhouse","Sazerac Rye","Thomas H. Handy","Wild Turkey","Pikesville"],"shelf":["thomas-h-handy"],"chapters":["american-rye"],"canon":["sazerac","manhattan","vieux-carr","cocktail-la-louisiane"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What is a Kentucky-style rye, and what does the federal rule ask of any American rye whisky?","answer":"No legal category: a recipe just over the line, often about 51 to 55 per cent rye with corn making most of the rest, so rounder and sweeter. Under 27 CFR 5.143 rye whisky is at least 51 per cent rye, distilled at no more than 160 proof and stored in charred new oak at no more than 125 proof."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"What share of rye must an American rye whisky mash hold, at the least?","options":["51 per cent","65 per cent","80 per cent","95 per cent"],"a":0,"explain":"At least 51 per cent rye, under 27 CFR 5.143. A Kentucky-style rye sits just over that line; a rye of 95 per cent sits at the rye-heavy end."},{"id":"q2","q":"Which grain makes up most of the rest of a Kentucky-style rye's mash?","options":["Wheat","Malted rye","Corn","Oats"],"a":2,"explain":"Corn, with a little malted barley for its enzymes. That corn is what makes a Kentucky-style rye rounder and sweeter than a rye-heavy one."},{"id":"q3","q":"How does a Kentucky-style rye usually differ from a Monongahela-style rye?","options":["More rye, so drier and spicier","More corn, so rounder and sweeter","Aged in used oak rather than new","Distilled from malted barley alone"],"a":1,"explain":"More corn: Monongahela rye of western Pennsylvania was usually rye-heavy with little or no corn. Both are rye whisky in new charred oak under the same federal rule."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"Legal Information Institute: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/27/5.143"},{"title":"VinePair: Styles of rye explained","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/styles-of-rye-explained/"},{"title":"Flaviar: No one knows what Pennsylvania rye whiskey should taste like","url":"https://flaviar.com/blogs/flaviar-times/pennsylvania-rye-whiskey"},{"title":"Paste Magazine: Rye whiskey vs bourbon, flavour, definition and cocktails","url":"https://www.pastemagazine.com/drink/whiskey/rye-whiskey-vs-bourbon-flavor-definition-cocktails"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Rye whiskey","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rye_whiskey"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: The mashbills of Buffalo Trace","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/The-Mashbills-of-Buffalo-Trace"},{"title":"Tasting Table: Sazerac, the original and the Thomas Handy evolution","url":"https://tastingtable.com/688164/sazerac-original-and-thomas-handy-evolution"},{"title":"Difford's Guide: Sazerac (New Orleans style)","url":"https://www.diffordsguide.com/cocktails/recipe/3390/sazerac"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: American Rye","url":""}]},
    {"id":"monongahela-rye","name":"Maryland and Monongahela rye","respell":"muh-NON-guh-HEE-luh","family":"american","terms":["Monongahela","Maryland rye","Pennsylvania rye"],"neighbour":"kentucky-rye","law":"No legal category of its own: Monongahela, Pennsylvania and Maryland rye are historic regional styles, and no rule fixes their recipe. The whiskey is rye whisky under 27 CFR 5.143, the United States standards of identity: at least 51 per cent rye, distilled at no more than 80% ABV (160 proof), stored in charred new oak at no more than 62.5% ABV (125 proof) and bottled at 40% ABV (80 proof) or more.","made":"Monongahela rye, from the river valleys of western Pennsylvania, was usually made rye-heavy: rye with malted barley or malted rye and little or no corn, sometimes close to all rye. Maryland rye is often described as made lighter and rounder. Today's revivals in both states mash rye-heavy recipes or marry high-rye and lower-rye whiskeys, then age them in new charred oak like any rye.","taste":"Monongahela-style rye reads drier and spicier: black pepper, clove, fresh grass and rye bread over dark fruit, with a firm, drying finish. Maryland rye is often described as softer and sweeter, honeyed, with gentler spice. Both are rye first, with less of the corn sweetness of a Kentucky-style rye.","lines":{"s10":"Monongahela and Maryland rye are the old rye styles of Pennsylvania and Maryland: one rye-heavy and peppery, one softer.","s20":"Monongahela rye, from western Pennsylvania, was made rye-heavy with little or no corn: dry, peppery, grassy. Maryland rye is often described as softer and sweeter. Neither is a legal category; both are rye whisky under the federal rule.","s45":"This is rye in the Pennsylvania manner, the old Monongahela style, with its softer Maryland cousin beside it. Neither is a legal category: both are rye whisky under 27 CFR 5.143, at least 51 per cent rye in new charred oak. Monongahela rye, from the river valleys of western Pennsylvania, was usually rye-heavy with little or no corn; Maryland rye is often described as lighter and sweeter. Expect black pepper, clove, grass and rye bread over dark fruit, dry and firm through the finish. We pour it neat, or in a Manhattan or a Brooklyn. The Whiskey Rebellion of 1794 rose in this rye country.","sale":"This is rye in the old Pennsylvania manner, rye-heavy and peppery, with little corn to soften it. Try it in a Manhattan or a Ward 8, or neat with a drop of water."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn, so the pepper and the grain show plainly.","water":"A few drops of still water soften the spice and bring out fruit and rye bread.","rocks":"One large cube for a cooler pour; the spice holds as it melts.","glass":"A Glencairn neat; a coupe for the Manhattan and the Brooklyn; a rocks glass for the Old Fashioned.","classic":"A Manhattan or an Old Fashioned; a Brooklyn or a Ward 8 among the older rye classics."},"guest":[{"q":"What is Monongahela?","a":"A river in western Pennsylvania, and the name of the rye whiskey made along it in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries: rye-heavy, with little or no corn. Today it names a style, not a rule."},{"q":"How is Maryland rye different?","a":"It is often described as softer and sweeter than Pennsylvania's rye, lighter in spice. Both traditions faded after Prohibition, and distilleries in both states make rye again."},{"q":"Can I still find the old brands?","a":"Some names live on: Old Overholt began in western Pennsylvania and Pikesville in Maryland, and both are now made in Kentucky. Newer distilleries in both states make rye in the old manner."}],"compare":"Kentucky-style rye, also no legal category, sits just over the 51 per cent rye line with plenty of corn, so it reads rounder and sweeter. Monongahela rye leaned to rye with little or no corn, drier and more peppery; Maryland rye is often described as the softer of the two old styles.","benchmarks":["Old Overholt","Pikesville","Sagamore Spirit"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["american-rye"],"canon":["manhattan","old-fashioned","brooklyn","ward-8"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What were Monongahela and Maryland rye, and what does the law say about them today?","answer":"Historic regional ryes, not legal categories: Monongahela, the rye of western Pennsylvania, usually rye-heavy with little or no corn, dry and peppery; Maryland rye, often described as softer and sweeter. Today any such bottle is rye whisky under 27 CFR 5.143: at least 51 per cent rye, aged in charred new oak."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"In which state did Monongahela rye grow up?","options":["Kentucky","Pennsylvania","Maryland","Tennessee"],"a":1,"explain":"Western Pennsylvania, along the river Monongahela and its valleys. Maryland had its own rye, often described as softer; a Kentucky-style rye carries more corn."},{"id":"q2","q":"How was Monongahela rye usually made?","options":["Rye-heavy, with little or no corn","Mostly corn, with a little rye","Mostly wheat, with rye and barley","Malted barley alone, in pot stills"],"a":0,"explain":"Rye-heavy, with malted barley or malted rye and little or no corn. That is why it reads drier and more peppery than a Kentucky-style rye."},{"id":"q3","q":"In which year did the Whiskey Rebellion rise in the rye country of western Pennsylvania?","options":["1776","1794","1812","1865"],"a":1,"explain":"1794: protest at the federal tax on distilled spirits, passed in 1791, turned to open revolt that year, and a federal force marched west to end it."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"Legal Information Institute: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/27/5.143"},{"title":"Punch: The fall and rebirth of America's first great whiskey, Pennsylvania rye","url":"https://punchdrink.com/articles/the-fall-and-rebirth-of-americas-first-great-whiskey-pennsylvania-rye/"},{"title":"Tasting Table: The historic American whiskey you've probably never heard of","url":"https://tastingtable.com/894062/the-historic-american-whiskey-youve-probably-never-heard-of"},{"title":"Flaviar: No one knows what Pennsylvania rye whiskey should taste like","url":"https://flaviar.com/blogs/flaviar-times/pennsylvania-rye-whiskey"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: What makes Maryland rye so different?","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/american-whiskey/makes-maryland-rye-different/"},{"title":"Baltimore magazine: Rye whiskey makes a comeback in Maryland","url":"https://www.baltimoremagazine.com/section/fooddrink/rye-whiskey-makes-a-comeback-in-maryland/"},{"title":"VinePair: Sagamore Spirit and the Maryland rye renaissance","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/sagamore-spirit-maryland-rye/"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Old Overholt","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Overholt"},{"title":"VinePair: The history and legend of Old Overholt","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/the-history-legend-of-old-overholt"},{"title":"West Virginia archives and history: the Whiskey Rebellion","url":"https://archive.wvculture.org/History/settlement/whiskeyrebellion01.html"},{"title":"Observer-Reporter: Region caught spirit of making spirits long ago","url":"https://www.observer-reporter.com/news/2017/jul/08/region-caught-spirit-of-making-spirits-long-ago/"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: American Rye","url":""}]},
    {"id":"american-single-malt","name":"American single malt","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["American single malt"],"neighbour":"scotch-single-malt","law":"27 CFR 5.143, the standard published on 18 December 2024 and in force from 19 January 2025: American single malt whisky is made from a fermented mash of 100 per cent malted barley, mashed, distilled and matured in the United States, distilled at one distillery at no more than 80% ABV (160 proof), stored in oak barrels of no more than 700 litres, new charred, new uncharred or used, and bottled at 40% ABV (80 proof) or more. Straight American single malt adds at least two years in oak.","made":"Malted barley alone is mashed, fermented and distilled at one distillery. The rule names no still, so pot stills, columns and hybrids all qualify. Then comes oak of the house's choosing: new charred barrels for vanilla and cocoa, casks that held bourbon, sherry, port or wine, or new uncharred oak. Some houses roast or smoke part of the malt. There is no minimum age, and a bottle under four years old states its age.","taste":"Malt at the centre: cereal, biscuit, toffee and honey, often with orchard fruit. New charred oak brings vanilla, caramel, cocoa and baking spice; roasted malts can bring coffee and dark chocolate, and a smoked malt brings smoke. The range is wide, from light and fruity to dense and oak-rich, so the casks on the label are the guide.","lines":{"s10":"American single malt is whisky from 100 per cent malted barley, made at one distillery in the United States under a federal standard.","s20":"American single malt follows a federal standard in force since January 2025: all malted barley, one American distillery, oak barrels of no more than 700 litres. Expect malt, toffee and orchard fruit, with vanilla and cocoa where new oak is used.","s45":"This is an American single malt. Under a federal standard in force since January 2025, it is made from 100 per cent malted barley, mashed, distilled and matured in the United States, distilled at one distillery and aged in oak barrels of no more than 700 litres. Unlike Scotch, no pot still and no minimum age are required, and the oak may be new charred, new uncharred or used. Expect malt, biscuit and toffee, orchard fruit, and vanilla and cocoa where new oak is used. We pour it neat with water on the side, or long with soda. Distillers banded together in 2016 to ask for the rule.","sale":"American single malt is barley whisky made the American way, often with new oak laying vanilla and cocoa over the malt. Try it neat with a little water on the side, or long as a Whiskey Highball."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn, so the malt, fruit and oak all show.","water":"A few drops of still water open the fruit and calm a higher strength.","rocks":"One large cube if the guest asks; colder, the malt and fruit close up.","highball":"Long with chilled soda over ice, a Whiskey Highball that keeps the malt in view.","glass":"A Glencairn neat; a highball glass long; a rocks glass for the Old Fashioned; a mug for the toddy.","classic":"A Whiskey Highball first; an Old Fashioned shows the oak, and a Hot Toddy suits a cold night."},"guest":[{"q":"Is American single malt like Scotch?","a":"It shares the grain, all malted barley, and the one distillery rule. It differs in freedom: no pot still and no three-year minimum are required, and new charred oak is common, so vanilla and cocoa often run stronger."},{"q":"Does single mean one barrel?","a":"No. Single means one distillery, as it does in Scotch. A single malt may marry many barrels; a single barrel bottling says so on the label."},{"q":"How old is it?","a":"The standard sets no minimum age. Straight American single malt has at least two years in oak, and any whisky under four years old must state its age on the label."}],"compare":"Single malt Scotch, under the Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, is malted barley from pot stills at one distillery in Scotland, matured there at least three years in oak. American single malt shares the grain and the one distillery but asks no pot still and no minimum age, and may use new charred oak, so its oak often speaks louder.","benchmarks":["Westland","Stranahan's","Balcones"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["american-single-malt"],"canon":["whiskey-highball","old-fashioned","hot-toddy"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What does the federal standard ask of American single malt, and when did it take effect?","answer":"27 CFR 5.143, published on 18 December 2024 and in force from 19 January 2025: 100 per cent malted barley, mashed, distilled and matured in the United States, distilled at one distillery at no more than 160 proof, oak barrels of no more than 700 litres, bottled at 80 proof or more."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"Up to what barrel size does the American single malt standard allow the whisky to be stored?","options":["250 litres","500 litres","700 litres","1,000 litres"],"a":2,"explain":"700 litres, the same cap the Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009 set for Scotch. The oak may be new charred, new uncharred or used, a freedom bourbon does not have."},{"id":"q2","q":"On what date did the American single malt standard of identity take effect?","options":["1 January 2024","18 December 2024","19 January 2025","1 July 2025"],"a":2,"explain":"19 January 2025. TTB proposed the standard in July 2022 and published the final rule on 18 December 2024."},{"id":"q3","q":"Which kind of still does the American single malt standard require?","options":["Pot stills only","Column stills only","A pot still and a doubler","No particular still"],"a":3,"explain":"No particular still: the rule asks for 100 per cent malted barley, one distillery and no more than 160 proof off the still. Single malt Scotch, by contrast, must come from pot stills."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"Federal Register, 18 December 2024: Establishing a standard of identity for American single malt whisky","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2024-12-18/html/2024-29938.htm"},{"title":"TTB: TTB establishes American single malt whisky standard of identity","url":"https://www.ttb.gov/public-information/featured-stories/ttb-establishes-american-single-malt-whisky-standard-identity"},{"title":"Federal Register, 29 July 2022: the proposed standard, Notice No. 213","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2022-07-29/html/2022-16244.htm"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: At long last, American single malt is now an official category","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/asm-ttb-news"},{"title":"Difford's Guide: American single malt whiskey","url":"https://www.diffordsguide.com/encyclopedia/4180/bws/american-single-malt-whiskey"},{"title":"Distillery Trail: TTB proposes a standard of identity for American single malt whiskey","url":"https://www.distillerytrail.com/blog/ttb-proposes-standard-of-identity-for-american-single-malt-whiskey-commenting-period-now-open/"},{"title":"Alcohol Professor: Why American single malt is becoming an official designation","url":"https://www.alcoholprofessor.com/blog-posts/american-single-malt-whiskey-designation"},{"title":"Uproxx: Scotch against American single malt in a blind tasting","url":"https://uproxx.com/life/scotch-vs-american-single-malt-whisky"},{"title":"legislation.gov.uk: The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, regulation 3","url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/2890/regulation/3/made"},{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.74, Statements of age","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-E/section-5.74"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: American Single Malt","url":""}]},
    {"id":"corn-whiskey","name":"Corn whiskey","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["corn whiskey"],"neighbour":"bourbon","law":"27 CFR 5.143: corn whisky is distilled at no more than 80% ABV (160 proof) from a fermented mash of at least 80 per cent corn. It is the one whisky that need not be stored in oak; if it is, the oak is used or new uncharred, filled at no more than 62.5% ABV (125 proof), and the spirit is never treated with charred wood. Straight corn whisky has at least two years in that oak. All whisky is bottled at 40% ABV (80 proof) or more.","made":"A mash of at least 80 per cent corn, the rest small grains, is cooked, fermented and distilled to no more than 160 proof. Much of it is bottled young or unaged, clear and full of grain. Aged corn whiskey goes into used barrels, very often ones that held bourbon, or into new uncharred oak, so the colour stays pale and the oak gentle. A bonded bottle, Mellow Corn among them, has at least four years at 100 proof.","taste":"Unaged, it is sweet with grain: fresh corn, cornbread, buttered popcorn and an oily, light body. Aged in used barrels it turns pale gold, with butterscotch, cereal and soft oak, and usually less vanilla and spice than bourbon. Some taste close to a light bourbon, others nearer a grain whisky.","lines":{"s10":"Corn whiskey is American whiskey from a mash of at least 80 per cent corn, sold young or aged in used or uncharred oak.","s20":"Corn whiskey starts with at least 80 per cent corn under the federal rule and, if aged, goes into used or uncharred oak, never charred new barrels. Expect sweet corn, cornbread and butterscotch, pale and gentle.","s45":"This is a corn whiskey, an American type under the federal rule: a mash of at least 80 per cent corn, distilled to no more than 160 proof. It need not be aged at all; if it is, it rests in used or uncharred new oak, never charred new barrels, and that is the line between it and bourbon. Expect sweet corn, cornbread and buttered popcorn when young, butterscotch and soft oak when aged, pale in the glass. We pour it on the rocks, long with ginger ale, or in a Hot Toddy. Southern moonshine was largely unaged corn whiskey, and many legal jars still are.","sale":"Corn whiskey is at least 80 per cent corn and light on the oak, so the sweet grain leads. Try it long with ginger ale as a Whiskey Ginger, or warm in a Hot Toddy."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn for an aged bottle, so the butterscotch and grain show.","water":"A few drops of still water soften a bonded 100 proof and bring up the sweet corn.","rocks":"On the rocks suits it, young or aged; one large cube keeps it cold without drowning it.","highball":"Long with ginger ale over ice, a Whiskey Ginger; soda for a drier glass.","glass":"A Glencairn neat; a rocks glass for ice and the sour; a highball glass long; a mug for the toddy.","classic":"A Whiskey Ginger first; a Hot Toddy on a cold night, or a Whiskey Sour."},"guest":[{"q":"Is corn whiskey the same as moonshine?","a":"Not always. Moonshine has no federal definition, so a legal jar is labelled by its type: often corn whiskey, sometimes unaged whiskey or neutral spirits. Corn whiskey on the label means at least 80 per cent corn."},{"q":"Isn't bourbon made from corn too?","a":"Yes, at least 51 per cent, but bourbon must age in charred new oak. Corn whiskey needs at least 80 per cent corn and may never touch charred new oak, so a high-corn bourbon stays bourbon."},{"q":"Why is it so pale?","a":"Used barrels and uncharred oak give far less colour than bourbon's charred new oak, and much corn whiskey is bottled young or unaged. The flavour leans to grain rather than vanilla and caramel."}],"compare":"Bourbon, under the same rule, needs at least 51 per cent corn and must age in charred new oak. Corn whiskey needs at least 80 per cent corn and may rest only in used or uncharred oak, if at all, so it is paler and grainier, with less vanilla and caramel.","benchmarks":["Mellow Corn","Georgia Moon","Balcones"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["american-labels","bourbon"],"canon":["whiskey-ginger","hot-toddy","whiskey-sour"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What does the federal rule ask of corn whiskey, and how does its barrel differ from bourbon's?","answer":"27 CFR 5.143: at least 80 per cent corn, distilled at no more than 160 proof; no ageing required, but any oak is used or new uncharred, filled at no more than 125 proof, never charred wood. Bourbon must go into charred new oak. Straight corn whiskey has at least two years."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"What minimum share of corn must a corn whiskey mash hold under 27 CFR 5.143?","options":["51 per cent","70 per cent","80 per cent","100 per cent"],"a":2,"explain":"80 per cent. Bourbon needs only 51 per cent corn, and a bourbon with 80 per cent or more corn stays bourbon because it ages in charred new oak."},{"id":"q2","q":"If corn whiskey is aged, which barrels may it rest in?","options":["Charred new oak only","Used or uncharred new oak","Former sherry casks only","Any oak, charred or not"],"a":1,"explain":"Used or uncharred new oak, filled at no more than 125 proof, and never treated with charred wood. Used bourbon barrels are a common choice."},{"id":"q3","q":"How long must a straight corn whiskey rest in oak?","options":["At least four years","At least three years","No time at all","At least two years"],"a":3,"explain":"At least two years in used or uncharred new oak. Plain corn whiskey needs no ageing at all; a bottled-in-bond one, Mellow Corn among them, has at least four."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"TTB: barrel and storage requirements by whisky type","url":"https://www.ttb.gov/media/73331/download"},{"title":"American Whiskey Magazine: five things you didn't know about corn whiskey","url":"https://www.americanwhiskeymag.com/articles/5-things-you-didnt-know-about-1-corn-whiskey/"},{"title":"Difford's Guide: Mellow Corn Bottled in Bond Kentucky Straight Corn Whiskey","url":"https://www.diffordsguide.com/beer-wine-spirits/5446/mellow-corn-bottled-in-bond"},{"title":"The Bourbon Culture: Mellow Corn Bottled in Bond review","url":"https://thebourbonculture.com/whiskey-reviews/mellow-corn-bottled-in-bond-review/"},{"title":"Imbibe: Balcones Baby Blue corn whisky","url":"https://imbibemagazine.com/?p=67990"},{"title":"Distiller: An attempt at defining moonshine","url":"https://blog.distiller.com/moonshine-definition/"},{"title":"InsideHook: Your guide to legal moonshine","url":"https://www.insidehook.com/food/your-guide-to-legal-moonshine/amp"},{"title":"Saveur: White lightning","url":"https://www.saveur.com/article/Wine-and-Drink/White-Whiskey-Moonshine"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: American Labels","url":""},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Bourbon","url":""}]},
    {"id":"wheat-whiskey","name":"Wheat whiskey","respell":"","family":"american","terms":["wheat whiskey"],"neighbour":"wheated-bourbon","law":"27 CFR 5.143: wheat whisky is distilled at no more than 80% ABV (160 proof) from a fermented mash of at least 51 per cent wheat, stored at no more than 62.5% ABV (125 proof) in charred new oak containers, and bottled at 40% ABV (80 proof) or more. Straight wheat whisky has at least two years in that oak. It is a type of its own beside bourbon and rye, not a kind of bourbon.","made":"Wheat leads the mash, at least 51 per cent, with corn, malted barley and sometimes rye making up the rest; a few houses use wheat alone. Bernheim Original is published at 51 per cent wheat, Woodford Reserve's wheat whiskey at 52 per cent with corn, malted barley and rye, and Dry Fly's at 100 per cent. It is distilled and aged in charred new oak as bourbon is, so barrel and age shape it as much as the grain.","taste":"Wheat is often found softer and rounder: fresh bread, toasted biscuit, honey and toffee, with orchard fruit and gentle baking spice over the vanilla and caramel of new oak. Usually less pepper than a rye; the grain is one influence among yeast, barrel, proof and age, so a long-aged or high proof bottle can still run rich and warm.","lines":{"s10":"Wheat whiskey is American whiskey made from a mash of at least 51 per cent wheat and aged in charred new oak.","s20":"Wheat whiskey is its own federal type: at least 51 per cent wheat, distilled to no more than 160 proof and aged in charred new oak. It is often found soft and round, with bread, honey and toffee.","s45":"This is a wheat whiskey, its own type under the federal rule: at least 51 per cent wheat in the mash where bourbon would have corn, distilled to no more than 160 proof and aged in charred new oak, as bourbon is. Wheat is often found soft and round: fresh bread and toasted biscuit, honey and toffee, orchard fruit and gentle spice over the vanilla of the barrel. We pour it neat with a few drops of water, or in a Whiskey Sour or a Gold Rush, where the softness carries the citrus. Bernheim Original, a Kentucky straight wheat whiskey, first appeared in 2005.","sale":"Wheat whiskey puts wheat where bourbon puts corn, so it is often soft, bready and honeyed. Try it neat with a drop of water, or in a Gold Rush with honey and lemon."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature in a Glencairn, so the bread, honey and fruit come forward.","water":"A few drops of still water open the fruit and soften a higher strength.","rocks":"One large cube; small ice waters a soft whiskey down too fast.","highball":"Long with chilled soda over ice, a Whiskey Highball that keeps the grain gentle and clear.","glass":"A Glencairn neat; a rocks glass for the cube, the sour and the Gold Rush; a highball glass long.","classic":"A Whiskey Sour or a Gold Rush, where honey meets the wheat; a Whiskey Highball long."},"guest":[{"q":"Is wheat whiskey the same as wheated bourbon?","a":"No. Wheat whiskey is at least 51 per cent wheat, a type of its own. Wheated bourbon is at least 51 per cent corn, with wheat as the second grain in place of rye."},{"q":"Is it sweeter?","a":"It is often found softer and rounder, bread and honey rather than pepper. That comes from the grain and the charred new oak; proof and age shape it as much as the wheat does."},{"q":"How is it different from a rye?","a":"A rye whiskey, at least 51 per cent rye, leans to pepper, dill and a drier finish. Wheat whiskey is often softer: bread, honey and toffee, with gentle spice. Both age in charred new oak."}],"compare":"Wheated bourbon is bourbon, at least 51 per cent corn, with wheat as its second grain. Wheat whiskey turns that round, at least 51 per cent wheat, under the same 160 proof, 125 proof and charred new oak limits. The wheated bourbon often shows more sweet corn and caramel, the wheat whiskey more bread and biscuit.","benchmarks":["Bernheim","Woodford Reserve","Dry Fly"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["american-labels"],"canon":["whiskey-sour","gold-rush","whiskey-highball"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What does the federal rule ask of wheat whiskey, and how does it differ from wheated bourbon?","answer":"27 CFR 5.143: at least 51 per cent wheat, distilled at no more than 160 proof, charred new oak filled at no more than 125 proof, bottled at 80 proof or more, straight at two years. Wheated bourbon is at least 51 per cent corn with wheat as the second grain."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"What minimum share of wheat must a wheat whiskey mash hold under 27 CFR 5.143?","options":["20 per cent","51 per cent","80 per cent","100 per cent"],"a":1,"explain":"51 per cent, the same majority the rule asks of corn for bourbon and of rye for rye whisky. A wheated bourbon carries far less wheat, as its second grain."},{"id":"q2","q":"Which barrels must a wheat whiskey be stored in?","options":["Used bourbon barrels","New uncharred oak","Charred new oak","Any oak up to 700 litres"],"a":2,"explain":"Charred new oak, filled at no more than 125 proof, the same barrel rule as bourbon and rye. Used or uncharred new oak is corn whiskey's barrel."},{"id":"q3","q":"In which year did Bernheim Original, a Kentucky straight wheat whiskey, first appear?","options":["1935","1964","1999","2005"],"a":3,"explain":"2005, with a recipe published at 51 per cent wheat, exactly the share the rule asks. Stitzel-Weller opened in 1935, and in 1964 Congress named bourbon a distinctive product of the United States."}],"sources":[{"title":"eCFR: 27 CFR 5.143, Whisky","url":"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-27/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/subpart-I/section-5.143"},{"title":"Breaking Bourbon: Bernheim Original wheat whiskey review","url":"https://breakingbourbon.com/review/bernheim-original-wheat-whiskey"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: Heaven Hill celebrates 20 years of Bernheim with a limited edition wheat whiskey","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/whiskey-news/heaven-hill-celebrates-20-years-of-bernheim-with-limited-edition-wheat-whiskey/"},{"title":"Difford's Guide: Bernheim Original 7 Year Old Kentucky Straight Wheat Whiskey","url":"https://www.diffordsguide.com/beer-wine-spirits/4538/bernheim-original"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: Woodford Reserve adds a wheat whiskey to its line up","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/american-whiskey/woodford-reserve-adds-a-wheat-whiskey-to-its-line-up/"},{"title":"The Manual: Woodford Reserve wheat whiskey","url":"https://themanual.com/food-and-drink/woodford-reserve-wheat-whiskey"},{"title":"Flaviar: Dry Fly Straight Washington Wheat Whiskey","url":"https://flaviar.com/dry-fly/dry-fly-washington-wheat-whiskey"},{"title":"Kysela: Dry Fly wheat whiskey","url":"https://kysela.com/dry-fly-wheat-whiskey.html"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: American Labels","url":""}]},
    {"id":"scotch-single-malt","name":"Single malt Scotch","respell":"","family":"scotch","terms":["single malt Scotch","single malt","Scotch single malt"],"neighbour":"blended-malt","law":"The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009 define single malt Scotch whisky: distilled at one distillery, in one or more batches, in pot stills, from water and malted barley with no other cereal. Every Scotch rule holds: distilled below 94.8% ABV, matured in Scotland at least three years in oak casks of no more than 700 litres, bottled at 40% ABV or more, with nothing added but water and plain caramel. Since 23 November 2012 it may leave Scotland only bottled and labelled, ready for sale.","made":"Malted barley is milled, mashed with hot water and fermented with yeast, then distilled in copper pot stills, usually twice; Auchentoshan distils three times. Drying the malt over peat smoke is a choice, not a rule. The spirit matures in oak, mostly ex-bourbon barrels and ex-sherry casks, and most bottlings marry many casks of the one distillery. An age statement promises that every whisky in the bottle is at least that old.","taste":"It ranges widely: often malt, honey and orchard fruit over vanilla from bourbon casks; dried fruit, nuts and spice from sherry casks; smoke, iodine and salt where the malt was peated. Texture runs from light and floral to oily and rich. The distillery and the cask decide, not the category.","lines":{"s10":"Single malt Scotch is whisky from malted barley alone, distilled in pot stills at one distillery in Scotland.","s20":"Single malt Scotch is all malted barley, distilled in copper pot stills at a single distillery and aged at least three years in oak in Scotland. Expect malt, honey and orchard fruit or sherried dried fruit, and smoke only where the malt was peated.","s45":"This is a single malt Scotch whisky. Under the Scotch Whisky Regulations it comes from one distillery in Scotland, made from water and malted barley alone and distilled in copper pot stills, usually twice, then aged at least three years in oak. Single means one distillery, not one cask: most bottlings marry many casks. Expect malt, honey and orchard fruit with vanilla from bourbon casks, dried fruit and spice from sherry casks, and smoke only where the malt was peated. We pour it neat with a jug of water beside it. In 1963 Glenfiddich began selling its single malt beyond Scotland, when blends filled most glasses.","sale":"A single malt is one Scottish distillery's own voice: malted barley, copper pot stills and the casks it chose. Taste it neat with a few drops of water, or long with soda in a highball."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature, so the malt, the fruit and the cask all show.","water":"A few drops of still water soften the alcohol and open fruit, honey and malt.","rocks":"One large cube if asked; cold mutes the aroma, so offer the water first.","highball":"With chilled soda over ice in a tall glass, a lighter malt makes a clean highball.","glass":"A Glencairn or nosing glass neat; a rocks glass for the cube; a highball glass for soda.","classic":"A Whiskey Highball with a lighter malt; a Hot Toddy on a cold night; the Blue Blazer as theatre."},"guest":[{"q":"Is single malt better than a blend?","a":"Neither is a grade. Single malt names one distillery and malted barley in pot stills; a blend adds grain whisky. Some guests want one distillery's character, others a blend's balance."},{"q":"Does single malt mean one barrel?","a":"No. Single means one distillery. Most bottlings marry many casks from that distillery; a single cask bottling says so on its label."},{"q":"Is all single malt smoky?","a":"No. Smoke comes from drying the malt over peat, a choice some distilleries make. Many single malts are unpeated and lead with fruit, honey and malt."},{"q":"What does the age on the bottle mean?","a":"That every whisky in it is at least that old. A 12 year old may hold older whisky, never younger, and a bottle with no age stated is still at least three years old."}],"compare":"Against a blended malt: both are all malted barley from pot stills with no grain whisky, but a single malt comes from one distillery, while a blended malt marries single malts from two or more. The single malt shows one house's character; the blended malt is built by a blender across houses.","benchmarks":["Glenfiddich","The Glenlivet","Macallan","Highland Park","Springbank","Lagavulin"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["scotch","blending"],"canon":["whiskey-highball","hot-toddy","blue-blazer"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What must a single malt Scotch be under the Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, and what does single count?","answer":"Distilled at one distillery from water and malted barley only, in pot stills, then aged at least three years in oak casks in Scotland and bottled at 40% ABV or more. Single counts the distillery, not the cask: most bottlings marry many casks."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"Under the Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, what may a single malt Scotch be distilled from?","options":["Water and malted barley only","Malted barley with some wheat","Any cereal, if pot distilled","Malted and unmalted barley"],"a":0,"explain":"Water and malted barley, with no other cereal, distilled in pot stills at one distillery. Unmalted barley belongs to Irish pot still whiskey; wheat or maize makes a Scotch single grain."},{"id":"q2","q":"Since 23 November 2012, in what form may a single malt Scotch leave Scotland?","options":["In oak casks of up to 700 litres","Bottled and labelled, ready for sale","In bulk tankers to licensed bottlers","In any container, once three years old"],"a":1,"explain":"Regulation 7 of the Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009: from 23 November 2012 a single malt may leave Scotland only in a bottle labelled ready for sale, so it is bottled in Scotland."},{"id":"q3","q":"What does the word single count in single malt Scotch?","options":["One cask","One harvest of barley","One distillery","One distillation run"],"a":2,"explain":"One distillery. Most single malts marry many casks of that distillery, and the barley and the runs may come from many years; a single cask bottling says so."}],"sources":[{"title":"legislation.gov.uk: The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, regulation 3","url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/2890/regulation/3"},{"title":"legislation.gov.uk: The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, regulation 7","url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/2890/regulation/7/made"},{"title":"Scotch Whisky Association: guidance for bottlers and producers, 2025","url":"https://scotch-whisky.org.uk/media/2387/guidance-for-bottlers-and-producers_2025.pdf"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scotch_Whisky_Regulations_2009"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Single malt whisky","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_malt_whisky"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Auchentoshan distillery","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auchentoshan_distillery"},{"title":"whisky.com: Auchentoshan distillery","url":"https://www.whisky.com/whisky-database/distilleries/details/auchentoshan.html"},{"title":"Food Ingredients First: The whisky that changed history","url":"https://www.foodingredientsfirst.com/news/the-whisky-that-changed-history.html"},{"title":"The Whiskey Wash: 5 things you didn't know about Glenfiddich","url":"https://thewhiskeywash.com/whiskey-articles/5-things-you-didnt-know-about-glenfiddich/"},{"title":"Master of Malt: Glenfiddich, pioneer of the single malt","url":"https://www.masterofmalt.com/blog/post/glenfiddich-pioneer-of-the-single-malt.aspx"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Scotch, grain, still and cask","url":""},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Blending and the language of the label","url":""}]},
    {"id":"single-grain-scotch","name":"Single grain Scotch","respell":"","family":"scotch","terms":["single grain Scotch","single grain","grain whisky"],"neighbour":"scotch-single-malt","law":"The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009 define single grain Scotch whisky as a Scotch distilled at a single distillery that is neither a single malt nor a blended Scotch. Scotch is distilled from water and malted barley, to which only whole grains of other cereals may be added, so wheat or maize is allowed, and the rule names no still. Every Scotch rule holds: distilled below 94.8% ABV, at least three years in oak casks of no more than 700 litres in Scotland, bottled at 40% ABV or more.","made":"Most single grain is wheat or maize, cooked and mashed with a share of malted barley whose enzymes turn the starch to sugar, then fermented and run through a continuous column still, which gives a lighter spirit than a pot still. A few houses run malted barley alone through a column. The spirit matures in oak, mostly ex-bourbon casks, which give much of its flavour, and grain whisky is the base of most blended Scotch.","taste":"Usually light and sweet: vanilla, toffee, coconut and soft cereal from ex-bourbon casks, with a clean, gentle finish. Long-aged grain grows richer, with butterscotch, honey and polished oak, and wine casks add red fruit.","lines":{"s10":"Single grain Scotch is grain whisky from one Scottish distillery, usually wheat or maize through a column still: light and sweet.","s20":"Single grain Scotch comes from one distillery in Scotland and is any Scotch that is not a single malt: usually wheat or maize with some malted barley, through a column still. Expect vanilla, toffee, coconut and soft cereal.","s45":"This is a single grain Scotch whisky. Under the Scotch Whisky Regulations it comes from one distillery in Scotland and is any Scotch that is not a single malt: usually wheat or maize with some malted barley, run through a tall column still, then aged at least three years in oak, most often ex-bourbon casks. Single means one distillery, not one grain. Expect a light, sweet glass: vanilla, toffee, coconut and soft cereal, richer with long ageing. We pour it neat, or long with chilled soda as a highball. Grain whisky is the base of most blends, made in stills descended from the one Aeneas Coffey patented about 1830.","sale":"Single grain is the light, sweet whisky at the heart of most blends, bottled on its own. Taste it neat for vanilla and toffee, or long with soda in a highball."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature, so the vanilla, toffee and cereal show.","water":"A few drops of still water bring up coconut and soft cereal.","rocks":"Over ice suits it; the light, sweet spirit holds its shape as it chills.","highball":"With chilled soda or ginger ale over ice in a tall glass, light and sweet.","glass":"A Glencairn or tulip neat; a rocks glass over ice; a highball glass for the long drinks.","classic":"A Whiskey Highball; a Whiskey Ginger with a lime wedge; a Hot Toddy with honey and lemon."},"guest":[{"q":"Is single grain made from one grain?","a":"No. Single means one distillery. Most single grains mix wheat or maize with some malted barley, and a few run malted barley alone through a column still."},{"q":"Isn't grain whisky just for blends?","a":"It is the base of most blended Scotch, but some is bottled on its own as single grain, sometimes long aged, and it shows what the blends are built on."},{"q":"Why is it lighter than a single malt?","a":"The continuous column still makes a lighter, cleaner spirit than a copper pot still, so the cask, usually ex-bourbon, gives much of the vanilla and toffee."}],"compare":"Against a single malt: both come from one distillery, but a single malt must be water and malted barley only, distilled in pot stills, while single grain is any other Scotch from one distillery, usually wheat or maize with some malted barley through a column still. The grain is lighter and sweeter, the malt fuller.","benchmarks":["Cameronbridge","Girvan","North British","Invergordon"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["scotch","blending"],"canon":["whiskey-highball","whiskey-ginger","hot-toddy"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What makes a Scotch a single grain, and what does single not promise there?","answer":"Distilled at one distillery in Scotland and not a single malt or a blend: usually wheat or maize with some malted barley, through a column still, aged at least three years in oak. Single means one distillery, not one grain."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"A Scottish distillery runs malted barley alone through a column still. Which category is the whisky?","options":["Single malt Scotch","Single grain Scotch","Blended malt Scotch","Blended Scotch"],"a":1,"explain":"Single grain. A single malt must be distilled in pot stills, so malted barley through a column still at one distillery is a single grain under the Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009."},{"id":"q2","q":"Which still do Scotland's grain distilleries usually run?","options":["Copper pot stills","Charentais alembics","Continuous column stills","Pot stills with a doubler"],"a":2,"explain":"Continuous column stills, descended from the still Aeneas Coffey patented about 1830. The rule itself names no still for single grain; it only says it is not a single malt or a blend."},{"id":"q3","q":"Which of these is a Scottish grain distillery?","options":["Lagavulin","Glenfiddich","Springbank","Cameronbridge"],"a":3,"explain":"Cameronbridge, in Fife, makes grain whisky in column stills. Lagavulin, Glenfiddich and Springbank are malt distilleries working copper pot stills."}],"sources":[{"title":"legislation.gov.uk: The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, regulation 3","url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/2890/regulation/3"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scotch_Whisky_Regulations_2009"},{"title":"Whisky Advocate: Single grain whisky","url":"https://whiskyadvocate.com/single-grain-whisky/"},{"title":"SMWS America: Cask curriculum, single grain explained","url":"https://smwsa.com/blog/cask-curriculum-single-grain-explained"},{"title":"Milroy's of Soho: How is single grain whisky made?","url":"https://milroysofsoho.com/blogs/the-whisky-librarian/how-is-single-grain-whisky-made"},{"title":"Food Republic: The difference between single grain and single malt Scotch whisky","url":"https://www.foodrepublic.com/1475819/difference-between-single-grain-malt-scotch-whiskey"},{"title":"Master of Malt: How to blend whisky, part one","url":"https://www.masterofmalt.com/blog/post/how-to-blend-whisky-part-i/"},{"title":"whisky.de: Blended whisky","url":"https://www.whisky.de/index.php?id=477&L=1"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Aeneas Coffey","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aeneas_Coffey"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Column still","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Column_still"},{"title":"Scotchwhisky.com: Whisky heroes, Aeneas Coffey","url":"https://scotchwhisky.com/magazine/whisky-heroes/20740/aeneas-coffey/"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Scotch, grain, still and cask","url":""},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Blending and the language of the label","url":""}]},
    {"id":"blended-malt","name":"Blended malt Scotch","respell":"","family":"scotch","terms":["blended malt","vatted malt"],"neighbour":"blended-scotch","law":"The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009 define blended malt Scotch whisky as a blend of two or more single malt Scotch whiskies distilled at more than one distillery: malt only, with no grain whisky. Each malt meets the single malt rule and every Scotch rule, matured in Scotland at least three years in oak casks of no more than 700 litres, and the blend is bottled at 40% ABV or more. The rules retired the older names vatted malt and pure malt, with labels given until 23 November 2011 to change.","made":"A blender chooses single malts from two or more distilleries, often from different regions, and marries them before bottling; nothing from a column still goes in. Some are built around one region, as Big Peat is around Islay malts; others set Speyside fruit beside Highland or Islay smoke. Some distilleries add a teaspoon of another distillery's malt to casks they sell, so that whisky can only leave as a blended malt.","taste":"It depends on the malts chosen: a Speyside-led blend brings honey, pear, malt and vanilla; one built on Islay malts brings peat smoke, iodine and salt; sherry casks add dried fruit and spice. A blender builds it for balance, so it often reads rounder and more even than one distillery's malt.","lines":{"s10":"A blended malt is Scotch made only from single malts of two or more distilleries, with no grain whisky.","s20":"Blended malt Scotch marries single malts from more than one distillery: all malted barley from pot stills, nothing from a column still. Expect malt, honey and orchard fruit, or smoke where Islay malts go in, built for balance.","s45":"This is a blended malt Scotch whisky, once called a vatted malt. Under the Scotch Whisky Regulations it is a blend of single malts from two or more distilleries, so every drop is malted barley distilled in pot stills, with no grain whisky, aged at least three years in oak in Scotland. A blender chooses the malts, often across regions, for balance. Expect malt, honey and orchard fruit with vanilla, or smoke and salt where Islay malts go in. We pour it neat with water beside it, or stirred in a Rob Roy. Since 23 November 2011 the old name, vatted malt, may no longer go on a label.","sale":"A blended malt is all malt from several distilleries, built by a blender for balance. Taste it neat with a drop of water, or have it stirred in a Rob Roy."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature, so each malt in the blend can show.","water":"A few drops of still water open honey, fruit and malt, and calm any smoke.","rocks":"One large cube suits a rounder blended malt; offer the water first.","highball":"With chilled soda over ice in a tall glass, with a lemon peel.","glass":"A Glencairn or nosing glass neat; a rocks glass for the cube; a coupe for the Rob Roy.","classic":"A Rob Roy or a Bobby Burns, stirred; a Whiskey Highball with soda."},"guest":[{"q":"So is a blended malt a blend?","a":"Of malts only. It marries single malts from several distilleries but holds no grain whisky, so it is all malt. A blended Scotch adds grain whisky."},{"q":"What happened to vatted malt?","a":"Same whisky, new name. The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009 retired vatted malt and pure malt, and labels had until 23 November 2011 to say blended malt instead."},{"q":"Why don't some blended malts name the distillery?","a":"Some distilleries add a teaspoon of another malt to casks they sell, so the whisky cannot go out as a single malt under their name. The label then says blended malt."}],"compare":"Against a blended Scotch: both marry whiskies from more than one distillery, but a blended malt holds single malts only, while a blended Scotch adds single grain whisky from column stills. The blended malt is fuller and maltier; the grain in a blended Scotch makes it lighter and softer.","benchmarks":["Monkey Shoulder","Compass Box","Big Peat","Johnnie Walker Green Label"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["blending","scotch"],"canon":["rob-roy","bobby-burns","whiskey-highball"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What is a blended malt Scotch under the 2009 regulations, and which older names did it replace?","answer":"A blend of two or more single malt Scotch whiskies from more than one distillery: all malt, no grain whisky, each malt meeting the Scotch rules. It replaced vatted malt and pure malt, which labels could carry until 23 November 2011."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"A bottler marries single malts from an Islay and a Speyside distillery, with no grain whisky. Which category is it?","options":["Single malt Scotch","Blended Scotch","Blended malt Scotch","Single grain Scotch"],"a":2,"explain":"Blended malt Scotch: single malts from more than one distillery, no grain. A single malt comes from one distillery; a blended Scotch needs grain whisky as well as malt."},{"id":"q2","q":"Which older label term did blended malt replace, unusable on labels from 23 November 2011?","options":["Vatted malt","Straight malt","Highland malt","Double malt"],"a":0,"explain":"Vatted malt, with pure malt, was retired by the Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009; labels had until 23 November 2011 to change to blended malt."},{"id":"q3","q":"A distillery adds a teaspoon of another distillery's malt to a cask it sells. What may the whisky be called?","options":["Single malt Scotch","Blended malt Scotch","Single cask Scotch","Blended Scotch"],"a":1,"explain":"Blended malt: malt from more than one distillery. The teaspoon means a cask a distillery sells cannot be bottled as a single malt under its name."}],"sources":[{"title":"legislation.gov.uk: The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, regulation 3","url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/2890/regulation/3"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scotch_Whisky_Regulations_2009"},{"title":"Osborne Clarke: Scotch whisky gets special protection","url":"https://marketinglaw.osborneclarke.com/retailing/scotch-whisky-gets-special-protection/"},{"title":"The Whisky Exchange blog: the end of vatted malt","url":"https://blog.thewhiskyexchange.com/?p=3446"},{"title":"Caskstrength: Vats all, folks","url":"https://caskstrength.blogspot.com/2011/11/vats-all-folks.html"},{"title":"The Whisky Shop: What is teaspooned whisky?","url":"https://www.whiskyshop.com/blog/what-is-teaspooned-whisky"},{"title":"The Scotch Malt Whisky Society: Just a dash","url":"https://smws.com/unfiltered/just-a-dash"},{"title":"Master of Malt: What is a teaspooned whisky?","url":"https://www.masterofmalt.com/blog/post/what-is-a-teaspooned-whisky/"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Douglas Laing and Co","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Douglas_Laing_%26_Co"},{"title":"Distiller: Blended malt whisky","url":"https://blog.distiller.com/blended-malt-whisky/"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Blending and the language of the label","url":""},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Scotch, grain, still and cask","url":""}]},
    {"id":"islay","name":"Islay","respell":"EYE-luh","family":"scotch","terms":["Islay"],"neighbour":"islands","law":"The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009 protect Islay as a locality, with Campbeltown. A Scotch may carry the name only if it was wholly distilled on the island; it may mature elsewhere in Scotland, and the rule asks nothing about peat. Every Scotch rule holds: distilled and matured in Scotland, at least three years in oak casks of no more than 700 litres, bottled at 40% ABV or more, with nothing added but water and plain caramel.","made":"Islay single malt is malted barley, mashed, fermented and usually distilled twice in copper pot stills at one of the island's distilleries. Most of them dry their malt over peat smoke, and the smoke's phenols cling to the grain and carry through the stills; Bunnahabhain and Bruichladdich make unpeated spirit as well. Most of it matures in ex-bourbon casks, with sherry casks for richer styles, and Islay malt also goes into blends.","taste":"In the peated malts: peat smoke, iodine, sea salt, tar and lemon over vanilla and pear from bourbon casks, with a long, drying, smoky finish; sherry casks add dried fruit and dark chocolate. The unpeated malts are a different glass: malt, nuts, honey and a little salt. Peat level varies from house to house.","lines":{"s10":"Islay is Scotch whisky distilled on the island of Islay, off Scotland's west coast: mostly peated, so smoke, iodine and sea salt.","s20":"Islay whisky is distilled on Islay, one of the two places the Scotch Whisky Regulations protect by name as a locality. Most of its distilleries dry their barley over peat, so expect smoke, iodine, sea salt and lemon; a few make unpeated malt.","s45":"This is an Islay single malt, distilled on the island of Islay in the Inner Hebrides, one of two localities the Scotch Whisky Regulations protect by name. It is malted barley, usually distilled twice in copper pot stills and aged at least three years in oak. Most of the island's distilleries dry their malt over peat smoke, so expect smoke, iodine, sea salt and lemon over vanilla from the cask, and a long, drying finish; a few make unpeated malt. We pour it neat with a jug of water on the side, and a little floats on a Penicillin. Bowmore, on Loch Indaal, dates from 1779.","sale":"Islay is the smoke and the sea in a glass: peat, iodine and salt over lemon and vanilla. Taste it neat with a few drops of water, or have it floated on a Penicillin."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature, so the smoke and the fruit under it both show.","water":"A few drops of still water calm the smoke and bring out lemon, pear and vanilla.","rocks":"One large cube at most; cold mutes the fruit and leaves only the smoke.","highball":"With chilled soda in a tall glass over ice, a smoky highball with a lemon peel.","glass":"A Glencairn or nosing glass neat; a highball glass for the soda; a rocks glass for the Penicillin.","classic":"A Penicillin, as the smoky float on top; a few drops in a Smoky Martini."},"guest":[{"q":"Are all Islay whiskies smoky?","a":"No. Most of the island's distilleries peat their barley, but Bunnahabhain and Bruichladdich make unpeated malts too. The smoke comes from drying the barley over peat, not from the island itself."},{"q":"Where does the smoke come from?","a":"Peat burned to dry the malted barley. Its smoke leaves phenols on the grain that carry through distillation; the cask then adds vanilla and fruit around them."},{"q":"How do you say it?","a":"EYE-luh. It is an island of the Inner Hebrides, off Scotland's west coast."}],"compare":"Against the Islands: Islay is a protected locality in the Scotch Whisky Regulations, while the Islands are an informal group whose whiskies are Highland in law. Both can be smoky, but most Islay houses peat their malt, while island malts such as Highland Park and Talisker range from heather and honey to pepper and smoke.","benchmarks":["Lagavulin","Laphroaig","Ardbeg","Bowmore","Caol Ila","Bruichladdich"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["scotch-regions"],"canon":["penicillin","smoky-martini","whiskey-highball"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What does Islay promise on a Scotch label, and does it promise peat?","answer":"That the whisky was wholly distilled on the island of Islay, a protected locality under the Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, with Campbeltown. It does not promise peat: most Islay distilleries peat their malt, but Bunnahabhain and Bruichladdich make unpeated spirit too. It may mature elsewhere in Scotland."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"Which other place shares Islay's standing as a protected locality under the Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009?","options":["Speyside","Campbeltown","Orkney","Skye"],"a":1,"explain":"Campbeltown. Highland, Lowland and Speyside are protected regions; Orkney and Skye belong to the informal Islands group, which is Highland in law."},{"id":"q2","q":"What gives most Islay single malts their smoky character?","options":["Malted barley dried over burning peat","Casks charred over a peat fire","Peaty water added at bottling","Sea air in the warehouses"],"a":0,"explain":"Peat smoke from drying the malt: its phenols cling to the grain and carry through distillation. Peaty water and sea air are popular stories, and Scotch allows only water and plain caramel to be added."},{"id":"q3","q":"Bowmore, on the shore of Loch Indaal, dates its founding to which year?","options":["1494","1823","1779","1815"],"a":2,"explain":"1779, by most accounts. The distillery stands in the village of Bowmore, on the shore of Loch Indaal."}],"sources":[{"title":"legislation.gov.uk: The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, regulation 10","url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/2890/regulation/10"},{"title":"legislation.gov.uk: The Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009, regulation 3","url":"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2009/2890/regulation/3"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Scotch Whisky Regulations 2009","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scotch_Whisky_Regulations_2009"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Islay whisky","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islay_whisky"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Bowmore distillery","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bowmore_distillery"},{"title":"Difford's Guide: Bowmore Distillery","url":"https://www.diffordsguide.com/producers/221/bowmore-distillery"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Scotland, map the place, question the stereotype","url":""}]},
    {"id":"irish-single-pot-still","name":"Irish single pot still","respell":"","family":"irish","terms":["single pot still","pot still","pure pot still"],"neighbour":"irish-single-malt","law":"The Irish whiskey technical file defines pot still Irish whiskey: a mash of at least 30 per cent malted barley and at least 30 per cent unmalted barley, with up to 5 per cent other unmalted cereals such as oats or rye, distilled in copper pot stills below 94.8% ABV and aged at least three years in wooden casks on the island of Ireland. Single means one distillery. A 2026 consultation on raising the other cereals to 30 per cent is a proposal, not the rule.","made":"Malted and unmalted barley are milled and mashed together, sometimes with a small share of oats or rye, then fermented and distilled in copper pot stills, three times at most houses, though the rule allows two. The raw barley gives an oily, creamy spirit with a spicy edge. It matures in wood, mostly ex-bourbon casks, with ex-sherry and other wine casks for richer styles.","taste":"A creamy, almost oily texture first, then spice: pepper, clove and ginger over green apple, pear, toasted cereal and honey. Sherry casks add dried fruit, nuts and toffee. The finish is long and warming, the spice lingering, and the whole is usually fuller and spicier than a blend.","lines":{"s10":"Irish single pot still is whiskey from malted and unmalted barley, pot distilled at one distillery: creamy, oily and spicy, with orchard fruit.","s20":"Single pot still is a style the Irish rules protect: malted and unmalted barley mashed together, distilled in copper pot stills at one distillery, usually three times. The raw barley gives a creamy, oily texture, with pepper, clove, green apple and honey.","s45":"This is an Irish single pot still whiskey, a style the Irish whiskey technical file protects: a mash of at least 30 per cent malted and 30 per cent unmalted barley, distilled in copper pot stills at one distillery, usually three times, and aged at least three years in wood in Ireland. The raw barley gives a creamy, oily texture, then pepper, clove and ginger over green apple, pear and honey; sherry casks add dried fruit. We pour it neat with a jug of water beside it, and it makes a fuller Irish Coffee. Before blends took over in the twentieth century, this was what Irish whiskey meant.","sale":"Single pot still is Ireland's own style: malted and raw barley together, so it is creamy and spicy at once. Taste it neat with a drop of water, or have it in an Irish Coffee that keeps its spice."},"serve":{"neat":"Neat at room temperature, so the cream and the spice both come through.","water":"A few drops of still water lift the green apple and the pepper.","rocks":"One large cube if asked; cold thins the creamy texture.","highball":"With chilled ginger ale over ice and a lime wedge, a long drink that keeps the spice.","glass":"A Glencairn or tulip neat; a rocks glass for the cube; an Irish coffee glass for the coffee.","classic":"An Irish Coffee, where its spice stands up to the cream; a Tipperary or a Hot Toddy too."},"guest":[{"q":"What does pot still mean here?","a":"In Ireland it names a style as well as a still: a mash of malted and unmalted barley, distilled in copper pot stills. Single means it all comes from one distillery."},{"q":"Is it triple distilled?","a":"Usually, at most houses, but the technical file allows double or triple distillation. What the rule insists on is the mash of malted and unmalted barley and the copper pot stills."},{"q":"Why the unmalted barley?","a":"It gives the style its creamy, oily texture and its spice. A tax on malt from 1785 is often given as the reason distillers turned to raw barley, though historians debate how much it caused."}],"compare":"Against an Irish single malt: the malt is all malted barley, while single pot still adds at least 30 per cent unmalted barley, which brings a creamier, oilier texture and more spice. Both come from one distillery and copper pot stills; the malt tends to lighter fruit and cereal.","benchmarks":["Redbreast","Green Spot","Yellow Spot","Powers John's Lane","Teeling"],"shelf":[],"chapters":["irish"],"canon":["irish-coffee","tipperary","hot-toddy","cameron-s-kick"],"recall":[{"id":"recall","prompt":"What goes into the mash of an Irish single pot still whiskey, and what does single mean?","answer":"At least 30 per cent malted and at least 30 per cent unmalted barley, with up to 5 per cent other unmalted cereals, distilled in copper pot stills, usually three times. Single means one distillery. Creamy, oily and spicy, with orchard fruit."}],"quiz":[{"id":"q1","q":"What minimum share of unmalted barley must a pot still Irish whiskey mash hold under the technical file?","options":["5 per cent","30 per cent","51 per cent","10 per cent"],"a":1,"explain":"30 per cent, beside at least 30 per cent malted barley; other unmalted cereals may make up to 5 per cent. A 2026 consultation proposes raising those other cereals to 30 per cent, a proposal only."},{"id":"q2","q":"Redbreast, Green Spot and Powers John's Lane are all distilled at which Irish distillery?","options":["Bushmills","Cooley","Kilbeggan","Midleton"],"a":3,"explain":"Midleton, in County Cork, where the stocks behind the style's revival were laid down."},{"id":"q3","q":"How many distillations does the technical file allow for a pot still Irish whiskey?","options":["Once only","Exactly three","Two or three","Four or more"],"a":2,"explain":"Two or three: the file allows double or triple distillation. Most houses triple distil, which is why many guests think it is the rule."}],"sources":[{"title":"European Commission, eAmbrosia: Irish Whiskey technical file","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/geographical-indications-register/eambrosia-public-api/api/v1/attachments/55944"},{"title":"Revenue: Whiskey verification checks","url":"https://www.revenue.ie/en/companies-and-charities/documents/excise/whiskey-verification-checks.pdf"},{"title":"Vinetur: Ireland opens review that could redefine Irish whiskey","url":"https://www.vinetur.com/en/20260629103448/ireland-opens-review-that-could-redefine-irish-whiskey.html"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Single pot still whiskey","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_pot_still_whiskey"},{"title":"The Whisky Exchange: Single pot still Irish whiskey, a complete guide","url":"https://www.thewhiskyexchange.com/inspiration/article/18566/single-pot-still-irish-whiskey-history-style-revival-the-whisky-exchange"},{"title":"VinePair: The history of pot still Irish whiskey","url":"https://vinepair.com/articles/history-pot-still-irish-whiskey/"},{"title":"Wikipedia: Green Spot (whiskey)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Spot_(whiskey)"},{"title":"Cask Atlas: Irish Whiskey, pot still, malt, grain and blend","url":""}]}
  ]
